hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"16336","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, that he used to dislike freeing the Christian."
"16337","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ata and Mujahid, they both said: The Jew and the Christian of the neck suffice for zihar."
"16338","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, he said: Everything in the Quran as believing, then the one who has prayed. What was not as believing, then what has not prayed suffices."
"16339","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about freeing the Jew and the Christian, is there reward in it? He said: No. And he disliked freeing him."
"16340","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: That I carry upon two sandals in the path of Allah is more beloved to me than freeing a child of zina."
"16341","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar told me, that al-Zubayr ibn Musa ibn Mina told him, that Umm Salih daughter of Tariq ibn Alqama ibn Murtafi' told him, that she asked Aisha, Mother of the Believers, about freeing children of zina. She said: Free them and do good to them. As for Ibn Uyayna, he mentioned it from Umar, from al-Zubayr, from Musa, from Umm Hakim daughter of Tariq, from Aisha the same. He said: And I think he said: She said: And treat them well."
"16342","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said about children of zina: Free them and do good to them."
"16343","From al-Aslami, from Abu al-Zinad, from Kharija ibn Zayd, that Zayd ibn Thabit freed a Magian boy of his, and freed a child of zina."
"16344","From Sufyan, about a man upon whom was a neck, then he bought his brother or a relative, and freed him. He said: It does not suffice him for his neck, because he cannot own him for a moment."
"16345","From Sufyan, he said: The infant who has not understood suffices for zihar and oaths, and the polytheist as well."
"16346","From Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Amr ibn Aws, from a man of the Ansar, that his mother died, and she ordered him to free on her behalf a believing neck. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him, and said: I own nothing but a black non-Arab slave girl who does not know what prayer is. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bring her to me. So he brought her, and he said: Where is Allah? She said: In the sky. He said: Then who am I? She said: The Messenger of Allah. He said: Free her."
"16347","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The disbelieving woman, do you see reward in her? He said: Yes."
"16348","From al-Thawri, from Sa'id al-Jurayri, from Abu Abd Allah al-Himyari, from Ma'qil ibn Yasar, he said: If you buy a soul, do not stipulate freeing for its people, for it is a knot of slavery. But buy it: if you wish you sell, and if you wish you give."
"16349","Al-Thawri reported, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, and Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Sha'bi, they both said: If you buy a soul, then stipulate freeing upon yourself, for it is not the sound one."
"16350","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, he said: Whoever owns a mahram relative is freed."
"16351","Ma'mar reported, from Qatada, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Whoever owns a mahram relative is free."
"16352","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from a man, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: Whoever owns a mahram relative is freed."
"16353","From al-Thawri, from Asim, from al-Sha'bi, he said: If the father, or the son, or the brother, or the mother is owned, they are freed."
"16354","Al-Thawri reported, from Isma'il ibn Umayya, from Ata, he said: If the brother, or the sister, or the paternal aunt, or the maternal aunt is owned, they are freed. And al-Hajjaj ibn Artah mentioned it, from Ata."
"16355","From al-Thawri, from Mansur and al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Alqama, he said: A man came to Ibn Mas'ud and said: A slave girl of mine suckled a son of mine, and I want to sell her. Ibn Mas'ud prevented him, and said: Would that he would call out: Whom do I sell as the mother of my child?"
"16356","Al-Thawri reported, from Salama ibn Kuhayl, from Mustawrid ibn al-Ahnaf, he said: A man came to Ibn Mas'ud and said: My uncle married me to his slave girl, and she bore me children, and he wants to enslave them. He said: That is not for him."
"16357","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: If the father owns the child, they are freed."
"16358","From al-Thawri, from Zakariya, from Amir, he said: If the father and the son are owned, both are freed, even if he did not speak their freeing."
"16359","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Whoever owns his brother is freed, even if he did not speak his freeing."
"16360","Hisham reported, from al-Hasan, he said: If the brother through suckling is owned..."
"16361","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, he said: If a man owns his brother through suckling, he is not freed. Al-Zuhri said: And the sunna has passed that the brother through suckling is sold. He said: Ma'mar said: And Qatada said: He is sold."
"16362","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin and al-Hasan, they both said: The brother through suckling is sold."
"16363","Al-Thawri reported, he said: Selling the mother through suckling is valid in judgment, but it is disliked for him. And the brother through suckling, his brother uses him for service and takes his yield."
"16364","Sa'id ibn al-Sa'ib reported, he said: A man among us bequeathed two necks, and named a price for them, but we did not find. So I asked Ata ibn Abi Rabah, he said: Combine it in one neck."
"16365","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri, about a man who says: If I buy so-and-so then he is free, then he bought him. He said: He is freed. I said to him: Then where is their saying: No freeing except in what one owns? He said: That is only one who says: The boy of so-and-so is free, so this is not valid. But if it was in his ownership, then he is free."
"16366","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, and from Qatada, from al-Hasan, they both said: Umra is that he says: It is yours for your life."
"16367","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar told me, that Tawus told him, that Hujr al-Madari told him, that he heard Zayd ibn Thabit saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Umra belongs to the heir. From Ma'mar, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, from Hujr al-Madari, from Zayd ibn Thabit the same."
"16368","From Ibn Abi Najih, from Tawus, from a man, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Made the ruqba for the one to whom it was granted, and the umra for the one to whom it was granted for life."
"16369","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is granted something for life, it is his."
"16370","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Habib ibn Abi Thabit told me, that he heard Abd Allah ibn Amr, a bedouin asked him, saying: A man gave a son of his a she-camel of his for as long as he lives, then it produced a herd. Ibn Umar said: It is his for his life and his death. He said: What if it was charity? He said: It is further from him than that."
"16371","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus told me, from his father, he said: I heard him saying: Umra is valid, and judgment is given by it."
"16372","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, he said: I heard Ibn Umar saying, while a bedouin asked him, saying: A man gave his son a she-camel of his for his life, then he bred it, and it became camels. Ibn Umar said: It is his for his life and his death."
"16373","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: I witnessed Shurayh when a man came to him and asked him about umra. He said: It is valid for its people. Then the man was silent a while, and said: How did you judge? He said: I did not judge, but Allah judged upon the tongue of His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever owns something for his life, it belongs to his heirs when he dies. From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Hasan, like the saying of Shurayh."
"16374","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Shurayh was approached about umra, and he judged that it belongs to its owner. He said: Did you judge for me, O Abu Umayya? He said: I did not judge. Muhammad judged for you, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"16375","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, that Sulayman ibn Hisham sent to him and to al-Zuhri while he was in Mecca, and asked them about umra. I said: It is valid for its people. He said: And al-Zuhri opposed him, and said: You two have differed upon me, so is there a scholar in Mecca? He said: I said: Yes, there is an old man I know none like, an old man more advanced in knowledge than him. He said: Who is he? I said: Ata ibn Abi Rabah. He sent to him that these two have differed upon me about umra, so what do you say about it? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged: That umra is valid. Then a man said: But Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan did not judge by this. He said: Rather Abd al-Malik judged by it among so-and-so's sons."
"16376","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Ikrima ibn Khalid told me, that Aws ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Sarh, brother of Banu Amir ibn Luayy, told him: We had a dwelling in the house of al-Hakam, then Abd al-Malik said in his rule: Your dwelling which is in the house of al-Asi. I said: It is not the house of the family of Abu al-As, but it is our house. It was ours in the Jahiliyya, then we submitted upon it. He said: It was yours only as umra. He said: I said: Whatever it was, it is ours by the judgment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: You spoke truth. Will you sell it? He said: I said: For wealth I will not sell it except for a house. He said: Then look at whichever of my houses you wish for its like. He said: I said: The house of Ayyub ibn al-Akhnas. He said: That is a house of the houses of Marwan, but other than it. He said: I said: The house of Hirmash. He said: It is yours. He said: Then I sold it to him for the house of Hirmash."
"16377","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Whoever is granted something for life, it is his."
"16378","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr told me, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: A woman in Medina granted for life a walled garden of hers to a son of hers, then he died, and she died after him, leaving children, and he has brothers who are sons of the grantor. The children of the grantor said: The garden returned to us. The children of the one granted for life said: Rather the garden belonged to our father for his life and his death. They disputed to Tariq, freedman of Uthman. He called Jabir, and he testified upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for umra belonging to its owner. Tariq judged by that, then wrote to Abd al-Malik informing him of that, and informing him of Jabir's testimony. Abd al-Malik said: Jabir spoke truth. He said: Then Tariq executed that. That garden belongs to the sons of the one granted for life until today."
"16379","Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr ibn Shu'ayb said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged that umra belongs to the one to whom it was granted for life. Ibn Jurayj said: And I was told from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Umra belongs to its owner, if he had taken possession of it."
"16380","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: The umra which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) allowed is only that he says: It is yours and for your descendants. But if he says: It is yours as long as you live, then it returns to its owner. He said: And al-Zuhri used to give fatwa by it."
"16381","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hisham ibn Urwa told me, from his father, that he narrated, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Any man who is granted an umra for him and for his descendants, it is his. Whoever among his descendants inherits it, inherits it."
"16382","From Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, he said: If a man gives one of his heirs something of his wealth for his life, or lodges him in it for his life, then it returns into the inheritance."
"16383","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man grants for life and stipulates upon the one he gave that when you die then it is free. He said: It is free, twice in succession. I said: A path among the paths of Allah? He said: Yes."
"16384","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Qatada, they both said: If he says: It is yours for your life, then when I die it is free. He said: No, and as I die it is free."
"16385","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: If I die then it is sold, then its price is for the poor. He said: And it is so, twice in succession."
"16386","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What if he says: it reverts to my heirs? He said: No, it belongs to the one he gave it to at that time, for his life and his death. I said: Then why do the two differ? He said: Because it is the condition of emancipation along with the life-grant."
"16387","From Ibn Jurayj, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: If he says: it is yours for your life, then it is for so-and-so, then it is as he said. Ali said: It is upon his condition. Mamar said: Qatadah said: It belongs to the heirs of the first."
"16388","From al-Thawri, he said: If he bequeaths and says: it is for so-and-so for his life, and when he dies then it is for so-and-so, he said: It belongs to the first, and he said: The latter gets nothing."
"16389","From al-Aslami, from Dawud, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The umra is valid and inherited."
"16390","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me about the umra and its established practice, from the hadith of Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Jabir ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari informed him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, decreed that whenever a man grants a man an umra for him and his offspring, and says: I have given it to you and your offspring as long as any of them remains, then it belongs to the one he gave it to, and it does not return to its giver, because he gave a gift in which inheritance takes effect."
"16391","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: If he gives him for a year or two years, then that is a loaned benefit he granted his brother, and it is not an umra."
"16392","From Mamar, from Ibn Shubrumah, he said: If he says: it is yours as a granted benefit as long as I live, or it is yours as a dwelling as long as I live, then it reverts to him. And if he says: it is yours as long as I live, without mentioning a granted benefit or a dwelling allowance, then it is valid for him and his offspring."
"16393","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata was asked, while I was listening, about a man who says: This slave-girl of mine is yours as long as you live. He said: This is the umra."
"16394","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan and Ata, they said: If he says: This house is a dwelling for you as long as you live, then it belongs to him and his offspring. And Qatadah used to say this and give fatwa by it."
"16395","From al-Thawri, from Ismail, from al-Shabi, he said: The dwelling-grant returns to its owners."
"16396","From Mamar, from Mansur, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: The dwelling-grant returns to its owners when the one who inhabited it dies, and settled it."
"16397","From Mamar, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: Concerning the dwelling-grant, its owner takes it back whenever he wishes, for it is only a loan."
"16398","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, that Hafsah, the wife of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, housed a freedwoman of hers in a house as long as she lived, then her freedwoman died, so Hafsah took back her house."
"16399","From Mamar, from a man, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, he said: The dwelling-grant returns to its owners when the one who inhabited it dies, and its owner may not take it back during it, and the umra is valid."
"16400","From al-Thawri, he said: If he says: it is yours as a dwelling, it returns. And if he says: it is yours, live in it, then it is valid for him forever; it is only like instruction from him forever."
"16401","From Ibn al-Taymi, he said: Ismail ibn Abi Khalid informed us, from al-Shabi, concerning a man who says to you: This house is a dwelling until you die. He said: It is for his life and his death."
"16402","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The ruqba is for one to say: It belongs to the survivor of me and you in death."
"16403","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Tawus, he said: The ruqba is for one to say: Take it, it belongs to the survivor of me and you."
"16404","From Sufyan, he said: The ruqba is for one to say: It is yours, and when you die then it returns to me."
"16405","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The ruqba is not lawful, and whoever is given anything as ruqba, it belongs to him."
"16406","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Tawus, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: There is no ruqba; whoever is given a ruqba, it belongs to the one it was given to."
"16407","Al-Thawri informed us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Whoever is given anything as ruqba, and whoever is given an umra, and whoever is given anything as umra, it belongs to him."
"16408","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Tawus, from a man, from Zayd, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, assigned the ruqba to the one it was given to."
"16409","From Qatadah, he said: The ruqba is valid."
"16410","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The ruqba is a bequest."
"16411","From Mamar, from al-Shabi, he said: Three women bought a house and said: It belongs to the divorced, the unmarried, and the needy among us. Then one of them died, so they disputed to Shurayh, and he said: This is the ruqba; when the first dies, the remaining two get nothing; it is upon the shares of Allah, mighty and majestic."
"16412","From Ibn Jurayj, from Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Ali, he said: The ruqba is like the umra."
"16413","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata informed me, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: There is no umra and no ruqba; whoever is given anything as umra or ruqba, it belongs to him for his life and his death. He said: And the ruqba is to say: This belongs to the survivor of me and you in death, and the umra is to grant it for his life, by granting his life. I said to Habib: Ata informed me from you about the ruqba. He said: I heard nothing from Ibn Umar about the ruqba, and I heard from him only this hadith about the umra, and I did not inform Ata anything about the umra. Ata said: If he gives for a year or two years naming it, then that is a loan he lends him; it is not an umra."
"16414","From Mamar, from Ata ibn al-Saib, he said: I was sitting, then a man passed, and it was said: This is Shurayh. So I stood to him and said: Give me a fatwa. He said: I do not give fatwa, but I judge. I said: A man gave a house to his child, then to his grandchildren, confined upon them, neither sold nor gifted. He said: There is no confinement in Islam against the ordinances of Allah, mighty and majestic."
"16415","From Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: al-Zubayr gave a house of his in charity, and made it confined upon his children and the children of his children, and it was upheld."
"16416","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me about charity of estates: None of the people of the charity excludes another of them, unless they have surplus of dwelling."
"16417","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade the gourd and the pitched vessel."
"16418","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: It reached me that it was forbidden to drink in the gourd and the hollowed trunk, and everything pitched, of skins and other than it. Nothing beyond that reached me. He said: I said: The lead vessel? He said: They claim Ibn Masud used to drink in the lead vessel."
"16419","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade the gourd, the hollowed trunk, the pitched vessel, and the green jar."
"16420","Mamar informed us, from Abu Jamrah al-Dubai, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas saying: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade the gourd, the hollowed trunk, the pitched vessel, and the green jar."
"16421","Al-Thawri informed us, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Awfa, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbid the green jar, meaning nabidh in the jar. I said: And the white? He said: I do not know."
"16422","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abu Qazaah informed me, that Abu Nadrah informed him, and Hasan informed them both, that Abu Said al-Khudri informed him: That when the delegation of Abd al-Qays came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, they said: O Prophet of Allah, may Allah make us ransom for you, what drinks are suitable for us? He said: Do not drink in the hollowed trunk. They said: O Prophet of Allah, may Allah make us ransom for you, do you know what the naqir is? He said: Yes, the trunk whose middle is hollowed out; nor in the gourd, nor in the green jar; and stick to the tied skin."
"16423","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu Harun al-Abdi informed me, Abu Said al-Khudri said to me: We were sitting with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he said: The delegation of Abd al-Qays has come to you. He said: And we saw nothing. Then we waited a while, and there they came and greeted the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to them: Is anything of your dates left with you? Or he said: Of your provisions? They said: Yes. So he ordered a leather mat and it was spread, then they poured the remainder of the dates they had. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gathered his Companions and said: You call these dates al-Burni, and these such, and these such, for the kinds of dates. They said: Yes. Then he assigned to every man of them a man of the Muslims, to lodge him with him, and teach him the Quran, and teach him the prayer. They stayed a week, then he called them, and found them about to have learned and understood. Then he moved them to others, then left them another week, then called them, and found them reciting and understanding. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we have longed for our land, and Allah has taught good, and we have understood. He said: Return to your lands. They said: If only we asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about a drink we drink in our land. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we take the palm-trunk and hollow it, then put dates in it, and pour water over it, and when it clears we drink it. He said: And what else? They said: We take these pitched skins and put dates in them, then pour water in them, and when it clears we drink it. He said: And what else? They said: We take this gourd and put dates in it, then pour water over it, and when it clears we drink it. He said: And what else? They said: And we take this green jar, and put dates in it, then pour water over it, and when it clears we drink it. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Do not make nabidh in the gourd, nor in the hollowed trunk, nor in the green jar; and make nabidh in these skins whose mouths are tied, and if it troubles you then break it with water. Abu Harun said: I said to Abu Said: Did you drink jar nabidh after that? He said: Glory be to Allah! After the prohibition of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him?"
"16424","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It was said to Ata: Is the watering-place of Ibn Abbas, in which nabidh is made, pitched? He said: Yes. And it was not in the time of Ibn Abbas; before that they used to be watered in cisterns of leather. These were introduced in the time of al-Hajjaj, after Ibn Abbas."
"16425","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: al-Hasan ibn Muslim informed me, from Tawus, that he used to say: Ibn Umar forbade jar nabidh and the gourd."
"16426","Ibn Jurayj said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, from Ibn Umar, that a man came to him and said: Did the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbid making nabidh in the jar and the gourd? He said: Yes; and his father used to forbid every jar and gourd, pitched and unpitched."
"16427","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, that he heard Ibn Umar saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbidding the jar, the pitched vessel, and the gourd."
"16428","Abu al-Zubayr said: And I heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade the jar, the pitched vessel, and the hollowed trunk; and when the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not find a skin in which nabidh could be made for him, nabidh was made for him in a vessel of stone."
"16429","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ayman informed me, from his father, that nabidh was made for Umar ibn al-Khattab in the large skin by Nafi ibn Abd al-Harith."
"16430","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ismail ibn Kathir informed me, from Mujahid, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade making nabidh in everything that is sealed shut."
"16431","From Mamar, from Thabit al-Bunani, he said: I asked Ibn Umar about jar nabidh, and he said: Unlawful. I said: Did the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbid it? Ibn Umar said: They claim that."
"16432","From Mamar, from Qatadah, and from a man, from Ikrimah, they said: The glass bottle and the lead vessel are disliked for making nabidh in them."
"16433","From Mamar, from one who heard Ikrimah, saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, split the drinking vessels on the day of Khaybar, because he found the people of Khaybar drinking in them."
"16434","From Mamar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ikrimah, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, entered upon one of his household, and they had made nabidh for a boy of theirs in a jug, so he poured out the drink and broke the jug."
"16435","From Mamar, from Qatadah, and from a man, from Ikrimah, they both used to dislike nabidh in stone vessels and in everything except the skins that are tied shut."
"16436","From Mamar, from a man, from Ikrimah, he said: Do not take cowhides as a skin in which nabidh is made that was not made for that; and if it was of sheepskins, then this is deceit, and Allah does not love deceit. He said: And it was said to Ikrimah: Do we drink sweet jar nabidh? He said: No. He said: Then thickened juice in the jar? He said: Yes. It was said: Why? He said: Thickened juice, if you leave it, only increases in sweetness, while nabidh, if you leave it, only increases in strength."
"16437","From Mamar, from Qatadah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: For me to drink a vessel of heated water that burns what it burns and leaves what it leaves is dearer to me than to drink jar nabidh."
"16438","From Mamar, from Aban, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: I asked Ibn Umar about jar nabidh, and he said: Unlawful. So I told that to Ibn Abbas, and he said: He spoke the truth; that is what Allah and His Messenger forbade. I said: And what is the jar? He said: Everything made of clay."
"16439","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abd Allah ibn Umar, he said: A man asked him and said: We take dates and put them in the pottery vessel, and he mentioned how he makes it. Ibn Umar said: The people of such-and-such land make wine from such, and call it such-and-such, until he counted five drinks, calling them wine, and counted five lands. Muhammad said: I remembered honey, barley, and milk."
"16440","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu al-Aliyah, he said: I entered upon Abu Said al-Khudri and asked him about jar nabidh, and he forbade me. I said to him: Then the juff? He said: That is fouler and fouler. I said to him: What is the juff? He said: Like the bridal seat, something with legs."
"16441","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, while on the road to Syria, was brought two flat vessels containing nabidh; he drank from one of them and turned away from the other. He said: So he ordered the other to be lifted, and it was brought the next day, with what was in it somewhat intensified. He said: So he tasted it, then said: Excellent, excellent; break it with water."
"16442","From Aban, from Said ibn Jubayr, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that he once prayed with his Companions, and when he finished his prayer a man called out and said: O Messenger of Allah, this is a man who drank. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, called the man and said: What did you drink? He said: I took raisins and put them in a jar, until when it reached its point I drank it. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: O people of the valley, I forbid you what is in the red, green, white, and black jar; let each of you make nabidh in his skin, and when he fears it let him dilute it with water. From Ibn Jurayj, from Aban, from a man, from Ibn Abbas, the like of it."
"16443","From Israil ibn Yunus, from Amir ibn Shaqiq, from Shaqiq, from Ibn Masud, that he gave him nabidh to drink in a green jar. Abu Wail said: And I saw that jar."
"16444","From Israil ibn Yunus, from Simak ibn Harb, from Qarsafah bint Ammihi, she said: I entered upon Aishah and she threw me a cushion, then a woman asked her about nabidh, and she said: We put the date in the jug and cook it, and make it nabidh and drink it. She said: Drink, but do not drink an intoxicant."
"16445","From Israil, from Simak ibn Harb, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Abi Ubaydah, she said: I used to make nabidh for Abd Allah in a green jar, with him looking at it, and he would drink from it."
"16446","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, he said: I heard Abu Jamrah al-Dubai saying: Anas ibn Malik used to drink jar nabidh. Abu Jamrah said: And Ibn Abbas said: Do not drink it, even if it is sweeter than honey."
"16447","From a man, from Shubah, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, he said: Ibn Masud, Usamah, and Abu Masud al-Ansari drank jar nabidh. From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, the like of it."
"16448","From Mamar, from Ata al-Khurasani, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I had forbidden you jar nabidh, so make nabidh in every vessel, but avoid every intoxicant."
"16449","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It reached me from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade us to make nabidh in a jar, or a gourd, or in a jar of lead, or a jar of glass, and that they make nabidh only in a skin tied shut."
"16450","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: One whom I trust informed me, that a man came to Ibn Masud and he gave him to drink from a jar. He said: Then I came to Ali and he asked for drink, and he was given drink from a jar. He said to the one who gave him drink: From where did you give me to drink? He said: From the jar. He said: Bring it to me. Then he went aside, then lifted the jar and struck with it so it broke. He said: If I forbade it only once or twice."
"16451","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Said, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on the pulpit, so I hurried but did not reach him until he descended. I asked the people what he said. They said: He forbade nabidh and the pitched vessel to make nabidh in them."
"16452","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Sulayman al-Ahwal, from Mujahid, from Abu Iyad, from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade the vessels. It was said to him: Not all people find a skin. So he permitted the unpitched jar."
"16453","From Bakkar ibn 000, from Khallad ibn Abd al-Rahman, that he asked Tawus about drink, and he informed him from Ibn Umar: That the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade the jar and the gourd."
"16454","From Abd Allah ibn Kathir, from Shubah ibn al-Hajjaj, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Zadham, he said: I said to Ibn Umar: Tell me what the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade of vessels. He said: He forbade the green jar, and it is the earthen jar; and he forbade the gourd, and it is the pumpkin; and he forbade the naqir, and it is the palm-trunk woven and hollowed; and he forbade the pitched, and it is the tarred; and he ordered drink to be in the skins."
"16455","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, he said: Umaymah narrated to me, she said: I heard Aishah saying: Is one of you unable to take every year the skin of his sacrifice and make it a skin in which nabidh is made? The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade, or she said: The Prophet of Allah forbade the jar to make nabidh in it, and two other vessels, except vinegar."
"16456","Mamar informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade al-zahw and ripe dates to be mixed, and raisins and dates to be mixed, and said: Each of the two is made nabidh alone. I said to him: What is al-zahw? He said: It is before the ripe date."
"16457","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata said to me: I heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying: Do not combine ripe dates and unripe dates, and dates and raisins, as nabidh. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, from Jabir, the like of the saying of Ata, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"16458","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade dates, raisins, ripe dates, and unripe dates, meaning: that they be made nabidh together."
"16459","From al-Thawri, from Muharib ibn Dithar, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying: Unripe dates and ripe dates are wine, meaning when they are combined."
"16460","Mamar informed us, from Thabit and Qatadah and Aban, all of them from Anas ibn Malik, he said: When wine was forbidden, he said: On that day I was serving eleven men, so they ordered me and I poured it out, and the people poured out their vessels with what was in them, until the streets were almost blocked by its smell. Anas said: And their wine on that day was only unripe and ripe dates mixed. He said: Then a man came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: I had an orphan's property, and I bought wine with it; will you permit me to sell it and return the orphan's property? The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: May Allah destroy the Jews; caul fat was forbidden to them, so they sold it and ate its price. And the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not permit him to sell wine."
"16461","Mamar said: And al-Zuhri informed me, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: May Allah curse the Jews; fats were forbidden to them, so they sold them and ate their price."
"16462","From al-Thawri, he said: One who saw Anas ibn Malik informed me that a bucket of unripe dates would be cut up for him."
"16463","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: When Anas wanted to make nabidh, he would cut from the date what had ripened of it and put it alone, and make nabidh of dates alone and unripe dates alone."
"16464","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar said to me: I heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah, or one whom I trust informed me from him, that ripe dates, unripe dates, raisins, and dates are not to be combined. I said to Amr: And is there other than that? He said: No. I said to Amr: Is it only forbidden to combine them in nabidh, and to make nabidh of them together? He said: Yes. I said: Then other than that of what is on the palm? He said: I do not know."
"16465","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed us, from his father: He forbade making nabidh of unripe dates and dates."
"16466","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed from Abu Ishaq: That a man asked Ibn Umar and said: Do I combine dates and raisins? He said: No. He said: Then why? He said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade it. He said: Why? He said: A man got drunk, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had him flogged, then ordered his drink to be looked at, and there it was dates and raisins. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade combining dates and raisins, and said: Each of the two alone suffices."
"16467","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah informed me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that he used to say: It has been forbidden to make nabidh of unripe dates and ripe dates together, and dates and raisins together."
"16468","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Did Jabir mention that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade combining two nabidhs other than what you mentioned? Other than unripe dates, ripe dates, raisins, and dates? He said: No, unless I forgot."
"16469","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata about other than what Jabir mentioned, of what is on the cluster and the palm, to be combined, and he refused. He said about the hulqan: Part of it is cut from part. He said: So I asked him about honey, whether things of dates and peaches are combined with honey as nabidh? He said: I hold that whatever strengthens another is upon that. He said: I said to him: Are dates and raisins combined, made nabidh then drunk sweet? He said: No, combining them has been forbidden. Ibn Jurayj said: And I say: Yes; do you not see that if a drink is made nabidh in the vessel which the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade, it is not drunk sweet? Abd al-Razzaq said: And al-hulqan is a stalk split, then two stalks placed in its hollow, then with dates."
"16470","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: What do you say about combining them at drinking, when they were made nabidh in two separate vessels? He disliked it and said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade it, as if he included that in the prohibition of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. I repeated to him, and he disliked it. He said: And I fear it will intensify. And Amr ibn Dinar said to me: I see no harm in that. Abd al-Razzaq said: And I see no harm in that."
"16471","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib: He disliked putting the dregs of nabidh into nabidh to strengthen it with the dregs. Al-natl is the sediment."
"16472","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah: That the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade making nabidh of raisins and dates together, and al-zahw and ripe dates together."
"16473","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq informed me, that Urwah informed him, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, that he heard him saying: The drink of crushed unripe dates alone used to be disliked."
"16474","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, from Abu al-Shatha: That the drink of unripe dates alone used to be forbidden. He said: And he also informed me from Ikrimah, freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Ibn Abbas, that he used to forbid drinking unripe dates alone. From Mamar, from a man, from Jabir, from Zayd ibn Aslam, I think he mentioned it from Ibn Abbas the like of it. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata, Amr ibn Dinar, and Abu al-Zubayr said: We do not know him to dislike it."
"16475","From Mamar, from Qatadah and al-Hasan; Mamar said: And it reached me from Anas, that they said: There is no harm in it. Mamar said: And I say: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Make nabidh of each of the two alone."
"16476","From Mamar, from a man, from Said ibn Jubayr, he used to say: If he crushes it by day and evening comes, let him not approach it. He said: And some of them say: Until it boils."
"16477","From Dawud ibn Ibrahim, he said: I asked Tawus about juice, and he said: Drink it in a skin as long as you do not fear it; when you fear it, break it with water."
"16478","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abd Allah ibn Murrah, from Ibn Umar, he said: Drink juice as long as its devil has not seized it. He said: And when does its devil seize it? He said: After three, or he said: In three."
"16479","From al-Thawri, from Husayn, that Abu Ubaydah ibn Abd Allah used to sell juice."
"16480","Hammam ibn Nafi informed us, he said: Tawus was asked about selling juice, and he was silent. Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid al-Sanani said: Whatever is lawful for you to drink is lawful for you to sell. So Tawus smiled and said: Abu Muhammad spoke the truth."
"16481","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: The steward of Sad ibn Abi Waqqas asked Sad about his land, as if asking his permission to press his grapes. Sad said to him: Sell it as grapes. He said: They will not buy it. He said: Make it raisins. He said: It will not keep. He said: Uproot it."
"16482","From Mamar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about a man who sold his grapes to one who presses them into wine. He said: There is no harm in it. Mamar said: And Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, that he used to dislike it."
"16483","Mamar informed us, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about a man who sold a sheep to a man wanting to slaughter it for his idol. He said: There is no harm in it."
"16484","Mamar informed us, from Layth, from Tawus, that a man bought wine and mixed water in it, then carried it to the land of India and sold it. He put the purse in the ship, and in the ship was a monkey. The monkey took the purse and climbed the mast, and began throwing a dirham on the ship and a dirham in the sea, until it finished it all."
"16485","Muhammad ibn Said ibn Rummanah informed us, he said: Hakim ibn al-Rafaf informed me, he said: Qays, freedman of al-Dahhak, and I came to Ibn Umar, and found him descended from the Jamrah heading to Makkah. Qays said to him: Praise be to Allah who granted us seeing you; you have seen the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and in seeing you is blessing; were you not in this state I would have asked you. He said: Ask whatever occurs to you. He said: So he said to him: A man frequented this House forty years, between hajj and umrah, and when he returns to his family he finds them made for him nabidh of these raisins; if water is poured on it it does not intoxicate, and if he drinks it as it is he gets drunk. Ibn Umar said to him: Come near me. So he came near him, and he pushed him in his chest until he fell on his backside, then said: You are he! No hajj for you and no honor. He said: I asked you only about myself; by Allah I shall never taste a drop of it."
"16486","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: Every intoxicant is unlawful."
"16487","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hasan ibn Muslim informed me, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent Abu Musa and his brother to Yemen as governors. They said: O Messenger of Allah, the people of Yemen drink drinks of theirs. He said: And what are they? They said: Al-bit and al-mizr. He said: And what is that? He said: As for al-bit: honey ferments; and as for al-mizr: a drink made of maize and barley. He said: I do not know what that is? Every intoxicant is forbidden to you both."
"16488","From Ibn Jurayj and Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, recited the verse of wine while addressing the people on the pulpit. A man said: Then what of al-mizr, O Messenger of Allah? He said: And what is al-mizr? He said: A drink made of grain. He said: Does it intoxicate? He said: Yes. He said: Every intoxicating drink is unlawful."
"16489","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Aishah, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was asked about al-bit, and said: Every drink that intoxicates is unlawful. Abd al-Razzaq said: Al-bit is honey nabidh."
"16490","Hisham ibn Hassan informed us, from Ibn Sirin, he said: I was with Ibn Umar, then a man came to him and said: I am a man who cannot digest food, so I order my family to make nabidh for me in a jar like this, and he gestured with his hand, and it digests my food. Ibn Umar said: I forbid you the intoxicant, little and much, and I call Allah to witness over you three times."
"16491","From Malik and Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: Every intoxicant is unlawful, and every intoxicant is wine."
"16492","From Ibn Abi Sabrah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Every intoxicant is unlawful."
"16493","From Abd Allah ibn Umar al-Madini, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: What intoxicates of a faraq-measure, a sip of it is unlawful."
"16494","Abd Allah ibn Umar informed us, he said: Amr ibn Shuayb informed us, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The little of what intoxicates in much is unlawful."
"16495","From Aqil ibn Maqil, that Hammam ibn Munabbih informed him, he said: I asked Ibn Umar about nabidh and said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, this drink, what do you say about it? He said: Every intoxicant is unlawful. He said: I said: If I drink of wine and do not get drunk? He said: Uff, uff! What is the matter with wine? And he grew angry. He said: So I left him until he relaxed, or he said: his face cleared, or he said: he spoke to those around him. I said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, you are a remnant of those I knew, and a rider may come and ask you about a thing, then take the tail of the word and strike with it in the horizons, saying: Ibn Umar said such-and-such. He said: Are you an Iraqi? I said: No. He said: Then from whom are you? I said: From the people of Yemen. He said: As for wine, it is unlawful, no way to it; and as for other drinks, every intoxicant is unlawful."
"16496","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: I forbid you the intoxicant, little and much, and I call Allah to witness over you."
"16497","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me: If a man drinks of the intoxicant what does not reach intoxicating him, painfully diluted with water, the hadd has become due upon him, even if he did not get drunk. I said: Nothing was revealed about it? He said: No punishment and no hadd, unless he repeats, then he is punished. I said to him: I found an intoxicating drink before me? He said: No hadd; he put him in the position of one about whom nothing was revealed."
"16498","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq said to me: There is no flogging for other than wine and tila of the intoxicant, unless he gets drunk from it; but if he drinks a sip of wine or tila, he is flogged."
"16499","From al-Thawri, from Said al-Jurayri, from Abu al-Ala ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade drinks. He said: It was said to him: They are unavoidable, or like this. He said: Then drink what does not befoul your minds nor consume your wealth."
"16500","Al-Thawri informed us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Dharr ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Abza, from his father, he said: I asked Ubayy ibn Kab about nabidh, and he said: Drink water, drink sawiq, and drink the milk you benefited by. I said: These drinks do not suit me. He said: Then wine, if that is what you want."
"16501","From al-Thawri, he said: Abu al-Juwayriyah al-Jarmi narrated to me, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas, or a man asked him, about al-badhaq. He said: Muhammad preceded al-badhaq; whatever intoxicates is unlawful. I said: O Ibn Abbas, what of the sweet, lawful, wholesome drink? He said: Then drink the lawful wholesome; after the lawful wholesome there is only the unlawful foul. Abu Yaqub said: We said to him: What is al-badhaq? He said: Something with which drink is strengthened."
"16502","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ismail informed me that a man gulped of a drink made nabidh for Umar ibn al-Khattab on the road to Madinah, and got drunk. Umar left him until he sobered, then had him flogged, then Umar diluted it painfully with water and drank of it. He said: And Nafi ibn Abd al-Harith made nabidh for Umar ibn al-Khattab in the large skin, while he was the governor of Makkah. Umar delayed until the drink passed its point, then it passed. He called for it, and Umar found it strong, so he put it in the bowls and diluted it painfully with water, then drank and gave the people to drink."
"16503","From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to avoid drink in the seasoned vessel."
"16504","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abd Allah ibn Abi Mulaykah narrating, he said: Wahb ibn al-Aswad narrated to me, he said: We took raisins of the purification raisins, and put much of them in our small skins, and little water. Umar did not meet them until it passed its point. When they met Umar, he said: Is there any drink? He said: We said: Yes, O Commander of the Faithful. They told him this story, that it had passed its point. He said: Show it to me. He tasted it and found it strong, so he broke it with water then drank. Abd al-Razzaq said: And all this is in the skins."
"16505","Mamar informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Ikrimah, freedman of Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, on the day he circumambulated the House, came to Abbas and said: Give drink. Abbas said: Shall we not give you, O Messenger of Allah, of a drink we made in the house? For hands have soiled this drink. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Give drink of what you give the people. He said: So they gave him to drink. Ibn Uyaynah narrated, then he called for water and poured it over it, then drank; and that drink was in the skins."
"16506","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mina informed me, that he heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad saying: It was forbidden to drink nabidh after three days."
"16507","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ibn Sirin, that Ubaydah used to say: People have introduced drinks I do not know what they are? I have had no drink for twenty years except water, sawiq, honey, and milk. And Ibn al-Taymi mentioned it, from his father, from Ibn Sirin, from Ubaydah."
"16508","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed from Mujahid, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, went to the watering-place, the Zamzam watering-place, and drank of the nabidh, and his face tightened. Then he ordered it a second time, and it was broken with water, then he drank of it and his face tightened. Then he ordered it a third time, and it was broken with water, then it was drunk."
"16509","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Said, he said: I heard Ibn al-Musayyib saying: Thaqif met Umar ibn al-Khattab with a drink, and he called them with it. When he brought it near his mouth he disliked it, then called for water and broke it, then said: Drink it like this."
"16510","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Ajlan, from Said ibn Abi Said, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: When your Muslim brother feeds you food, eat; and when he gives you drink to drink, drink, and do not ask; but if it troubles you, dilute it with water. From Abu Mashar al-Madini, from Said ibn Abi Said, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, the like of it."
"16511","From Zuhayr ibn Nafi, he said: I asked Ata ibn Abi Rabah about al-mizr, and he said: And what is al-mizr? A man beside him said: Al-ghubayra. He said: Every intoxicant is unlawful."
"16512","From Mamar, from a man who heard Hani, freedman of Uthman, he said: I witnessed Uthman when a man was brought with nabidh in a gourd he was carrying; he flogged him lashes, poured out the drink, and broke the gourd. And Abu Wail informed me that he heard it from Hani the like of it."
"16513","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from al-Saib ibn Yazid, he said: I witnessed Umar ibn al-Khattab pray over a funeral, then turn to us and say: I found the smell of drink from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, and I asked him about it, and he claimed it is tila. I shall ask about the drink he drank; if it was intoxicating I shall flog him. He said: I witnessed him after that flogging him."
"16514","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from al-Saib ibn Yazid, that he attended Umar ibn al-Khattab while he was flogging a man from whom he found the smell of drink; he flogged him the full hadd."
"16515","From Mamar, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, he said: When Umar found the smell of drink from a man, he flogged him lashes if he was of those addicted to drink, and if he was not addicted he was left."
"16516","From Mamar, from a man of the children of Yala ibn Umayyah, from his father, that Yala ibn Umayyah said: I said to Umar: We are in a land with much drink, meaning Yemen; how do we flog for it? He said: When the Mother of the Quran is recited to him and he cannot recite it, and does not recognize his cloak when you cast it among the cloaks, then apply the hadd to him."
"16517","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, claiming he consulted Ibn al-Zubayr, while he was the governor of Taif, about the smell, whether to flog for it? He wrote to him: If you find it from the addict, otherwise no."
"16518","From Mamar, he said: It reached me that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz was brought people who had drunk; some had got drunk and some had not. He had them all flogged. Mamar said: And it reached me that when an intoxicating drink is found with a man before him and he has not drunk it, then exemplary punishment."
"16519","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: Whoever drinks two sips of wine is flogged. He said: And if a man gives his son a like sip, he is flogged."
"16520","Abd Allah ibn Umar informed us, from Nafi, and Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd, she said: Umar ibn al-Khattab found wine in the house of Ruwayshid al-Thaqafi, and he had already been flogged for wine; so he burned his house and said: What is his name? He said: Ruwayshid. He said: Rather Fuwaysiq. Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Safiyyah, the like of it."
"16521","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The smell, while he is sane? He said: I flog only with clear proof; the smell may be from a drink in which there is no harm. He said: And Amr ibn Dinar said: I do not flog for the smell."
"16522","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz found people over drink, and found a cupbearer with them, so he struck him with them."
"16523","From Abd al-Quddus, from Nafi, he said: Umar found wine in the house of Ruwayshid al-Thaqafi, so he burned his house and said: What is your name? He said: Ruwayshid. He said: Rather you are Fuwaysiq."
"16524","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Umar exiled Ibn Umayyah ibn Khalaf for drink to Khaybar, so he joined Heraclius and became Christian. Umar said: I shall never exile a Muslim after him."
"16525","From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: Abd Allah ibn Masud was in Syria, and they said: Recite to us! So he recited Surah Yusuf. A man of the people said: It was not revealed like this. Abd Allah said: Woe to you; by Allah I recited it to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he said to me: You did well. While he was disputing with him, he found from him the smell of wine. Abd Allah said: Do you drink filth and belie the Quran? I shall not stand until you are flogged the hadd. So he was flogged the hadd."
"16526","Al-Thawri informed us, from Ata ibn Abi Marwan, from his father, that Ali struck al-Najashi al-Harithi the poet, then imprisoned him; he had drunk wine in Ramadan. He struck him eighty lashes and imprisoned him, then brought him out the next day and flogged him twenty, and said: I flogged you these twenty for your boldness toward Allah and your breaking fast in Ramadan."
"16527","From al-Thawri, from Abu Sinan, from Abd Allah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, he said: An old man who drank wine in Ramadan was brought to Umar, and he said: For shame! In Ramadan, while our children are fasting? He struck him eighty and banished him to Syria."
"16528","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ismail ibn Umayyah informed me, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, when he found a drinker in Ramadan, exiled him along with the hadd."
"16529","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Whoever drinks in Ramadan, if he has innovated a religion other than Islam, he is asked to repent; and if he is a sinner of the sinners, he is flogged, made an example of, paraded, and publicized; and the one who abandons prayer is like that."
"16530","From Mamar, he said: It reached me that when a man drinks an intoxicant, he is made an example of and disciplined."
"16531","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, who said: My brother Abd al-Rahman ibn Umar drank, and Abu Sarwa'ah Uqbah ibn al-Harith drank with him, while they were in Egypt during the caliphate of Umar, and they became intoxicated. In the morning they went to Amr ibn al-As, who was the governor of Egypt, and said: Purify us, for we became intoxicated from a drink we drank. Abdullah said: My brother mentioned to me that he became intoxicated, so I said: Enter the house and I will purify you, and I did not know that they had gone to Amr. Then my brother informed me that he had told the governor about that. Abdullah said: The people should not shave heads publicly; enter the house and I will shave your head, and at that time they used to shave along with the hadd punishments. So he entered the house, and Abdullah said: I shaved my brother's head with my own hand. Then Amr flogged them. Umar heard of that and wrote to Amr: Send Abd al-Rahman to me on a saddle. He did so, and when he came to Umar, he flogged him and punished him because of his closeness to him, then he released him. He remained healthy for a month, then his decreed time came and he died. Most people think he died from Umar's flogging, but he did not die from Umar's flogging."
"16532","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah and Ikrimah, who both said: Ibn Abbas said: Allah made shaving the head a Sunna and a rite, but you have made it a disgrace and added it to the punishment."
"16533","Al-Thawri reported from Abu Hayyan, from al-Sha'bi, from Ibn Umar, from Umar: The prohibition of wine came down, and it is from five things: dates, raisins, wheat, barley, and honey; and wine is whatever overcomes the mind. From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Umar, the same."
"16534","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Burdah, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, who said: Drinks are from five: wheat, barley, raisins, dates, and honey; and whatever you ferment and leave to mature is wine."
"16535","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibrahim ibn Abi Bakr informed me, from a man of Syria called Abdullah ibn Muhayriz al-Jumahi, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: There will be in the last of my Ummah people who deem wine lawful under a name they give to it."
"16536","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, who said: Abu Kathir informed me that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wine is from these two trees: the date-palm and the grapevine."
"16537","From Ibrahim ibn Abi Yahya, from Rabi'ah, from Ata ibn Abi Muslim, from Ibn al-Musayyib, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wine is from grapes, intoxicant drink is from dates, mizr is from maize, ghubayra is from wheat, bit' is from honey; every intoxicant is unlawful. Deceit and cheating are in the Fire, and sale is by mutual consent."
"16538","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani, from Abu Bakr ibn Hafs, from Ibn Muhayriz, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A group of my Ummah will surely drink wine under a name they give to it."
"16539","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever drinks wine in this world, then dies while drinking it without repenting from it, Allah will forbid it to him in the Hereafter. From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, the same."
"16540","Ma'mar reported, saying: Ata ibn al-Sa'ib narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr, from Ibn Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever drinks wine, his prayer is not accepted for forty nights; then if he repents, Allah turns to him. He said it three times. Then if he returns, it becomes due upon Allah to give him to drink from the river of Khabal. It was asked: What is the river of Khabal? He said: The pus of the people of Hell."
"16541","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I heard Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah narrating from Abdullah ibn Umar, that he said: Whoever drinks wine, Allah does not accept prayer from him for forty mornings; if he dies during the forty, he enters the Fire, and Allah does not look at him."
"16542","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, from his father, who said: I heard Uthman ibn Affan addressing the people, saying: Avoid wine, for it is the mother of evils. A man among those before you used to worship and keep away from women, then a wicked woman became attached to him. She sent to him saying: I want you to bear witness to a testimony. So he went with her slave-girl, and every time he entered a door she locked it behind him, until he reached a beautiful woman, and with her was a vessel containing wine. She said: By Allah, I did not invite you for testimony, but I invited you to lie with me, or to drink a cup of this wine, or to kill this boy; otherwise I will scream against you and disgrace you. When he saw there was no escape from one of what she said, he said: Give me a cup of this wine to drink, so she gave him to drink. Then he said: Give me more, so he drank and became intoxicated, then he killed the boy and lay with the woman. So avoid wine, for by Allah, faith and addiction to wine do not gather in a man's heart except that one of them is about to expel the other."
"16543","From Ma'mar, from Aban, from al-Hasan, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The wine-drinker will meet Allah on the Day of Resurrection when he meets Him while intoxicated. He will say: Woe to you, what did you drink? He will say: Wine. He will say: Had I not forbidden it to you? He will say: Yes indeed. Then he will be ordered to the Fire."
"16544","From Ma'mar, from Ja'far ibn Burqan: A woman among women asked Aishah about nabidh, and she said: You have asked me too much; if any one of you thinks that soaking her bread in water intoxicates her, let her avoid it."
"16545","From Ma'mar, from Aban, from a man, from Abdullah ibn Amr, who said: It is written in the Book that the sin of wine surpasses sins as its tree surpasses other trees."
"16546","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, from Talhah ibn Musarrif, from Masruq ibn al-Ajda', who said: The wine-drinker is like the worshipper of idols, and the wine-drinker is like one who worships al-Lat and al-Uzza."
"16547","Hammam reported from Khallad ibn Abd al-Rahman, that he heard Ibn Jubayr saying: Whoever drinks an intoxicant, Allah does not accept prayer from him as long as a drop of it remains in his bladder; if he dies from it, it becomes due upon Allah to give him to drink from the clay of Khabal, which is the pus of the people of Hell and their matter."
"16548","From Ma'mar, from Aban, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abu Dharr, who said: Whoever drinks an intoxicating drink, it is filth, and it makes his prayer filthy for forty nights; then if he repents, Allah turns to him; then if he returns to it the third or fourth time, it becomes due upon Allah to give him to drink from the clay of Khabal."
"16549","From Ma'mar, from Aban, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abdullah ibn Amr, who said: Wine is cursed, and its drinker, its pourer, its presser, the one for whom it is pressed, its seller, its buyer, the eater of its price, its carrier, and the one to whom it is carried."
"16550","From Ma'mar, from Aban, raising the hadith, who said: Evils were placed in a house and locked upon them, and its key was made wine; so whoever drinks wine falls into evils."
"16551","From Isra'il, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay', from Ubayd ibn Umayr, who said: Wine is the key to every evil."
"16552","From Ibn Abi Najih, from Ibn al-Munkadir, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever dies addicted to wine will meet Allah while He is angry with him, and he is like a worshipper of idols."
"16553","From Ibn Abi Yahya, from Ibn al-Munkadir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever drinks wine in the morning is like one who associates partners with Allah until evening, and likewise if he drinks it at night until morning; whoever drinks it until he becomes intoxicated, Allah does not accept prayer from him for forty mornings; and whoever dies with any of it in his veins dies the death of Jahiliyyah."
"16554","Umar ibn Rashid reported from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from a man, from Ibn Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah swore by His might and power: no Muslim servant drinks a draught of wine except that I give him to drink, for what he violated of it, of boiling water, tormenting him or forgiven; and he does not leave it while able to have it, seeking My pleasure, except that I give him to drink of it and quench him in the enclosure of holiness."
"16555","From al-Thawri, from Shaddad ibn Abi al-Aliyah, from Abu Dawud al-Ahmari, who said: Hudhayfah addressed us at al-Mada'in, saying: O people, check your slaves and know from where they bring you their taxes, for flesh grown from unlawful gain will never enter Paradise. And know that the seller of wine, its buyer, its pourer, and the one served it are like its drinker; and know that the seller of pork, its buyer, and its keeper are like its eater."
"16556","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, who said: Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As narrated to me, saying: On the Day of Resurrection the wine-drinker will come with blackened face, blue eyes, his side inclined, or he said: his jaw, his tongue hanging out, his saliva flowing onto his chest; everyone who sees him will loathe him."
"16557","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I heard Ayyub ibn Abi Tamimah saying: None of the people of Badr was subjected to hadd for wine except Qudamah ibn Maz'un."
"16558","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabi'ah informed me, and his father had witnessed Badr: Umar ibn al-Khattab appointed Qudamah ibn Maz'un over al-Bahrayn, and he was the maternal uncle of Hafsah and Abdullah ibn Umar. Al-Jarud, chief of Abd al-Qays, came to Umar from al-Bahrayn and said: O Commander of the Believers, Qudamah drank and became intoxicated, and I have seen a hadd of Allah's hudud, and it is due upon me to report it to you. Umar said: Who testifies with you? He said: Abu Hurayrah. So he summoned Abu Hurayrah and said: To what do I testify? He said: I did not see him drink, but I saw him intoxicated. Umar said: You have been over-scrupulous in testimony. Then he wrote to Qudamah to come to him from al-Bahrayn. Al-Jarud said to Umar: Apply the Book of Allah, mighty and exalted, against him. Umar said: Are you a disputant or a witness? He said: A witness. He said: Then you have delivered your testimony. Al-Jarud fell silent until he came to Umar in the morning and said: Apply Allah's hadd against him. Umar said: I see you as nothing but a disputant, and only one man testified with you. Al-Jarud said: I adjure you by Allah. Umar said: Either you restrain your tongue or I will harm you. Al-Jarud said: By Allah, it is not right that your cousin drinks and you harm me. Abu Hurayrah said: If you doubt our testimony, send to the daughter of al-Walid and ask her, and she is Qudamah's wife. So Umar sent to Hind bint al-Walid adjuring her, and she established testimony against her husband. Umar said to Qudamah: I will surely subject you to hadd. He said: Had I drunk as they say, you would have no right to flog me. Umar said: Why? Qudamah said: Allah the Exalted said: There is no blame upon those who believe and do righteous deeds for what they tasted, when they fear and believe, the verse. Umar said: You erred in interpretation; when you fear, you avoid what Allah forbade you. Then Umar turned to the people and said: What do you think about flogging Qudamah? They said: We do not think you should flog him while he is sick. He refrained for days, then one morning he resolved to flog him and said to his companions: What do you think about flogging Qudamah? They said: We do not think you should flog him while he is weak. Umar said: For him to meet Allah under the whips is dearer to me than for him to meet Him while it is upon my neck. Bring me a full whip. So he ordered Qudamah and he was flogged. Umar became angry with Qudamah and deserted him. Then he performed Hajj with Qudamah accompanying him in anger. When they returned from their Hajj and Umar stopped at al-Suqya, he slept, then woke from his sleep and said: Hasten Qudamah to me, bring him to me, for by Allah I see a comer coming to me; he said: Make peace with Qudamah, for he is your brother. So they hastened him to him, and when they came to him he refused to come, so Umar ordered that if he refuses they drag him to him. Umar spoke to him and sought forgiveness for him, and that was the first of their reconciliation."
"16559","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Abu Mihjan kept being flogged for wine; when it became frequent against them, they imprisoned him and bound him. On the day of al-Qadisiyyah he saw them fighting, and it was as if he saw the polytheists prevailing over the Muslims. So he sent to the umm walad of Sa'd, or to Sa'd's wife, saying to her: Abu Mihjan says to you: If you free him, mount him on this horse, and give him a weapon, he will surely be the first to return, unless he is killed. And Abu Mihjan recited as verse: Sufficient sorrow that horses meet with spears while I am left bound with my shackles; when I wish, the iron restrains me and doors are locked against me, deafening the callers. The other woman went and told that to Sa'd's wife, so she freed him from his bonds, and he was mounted on a horse in the house and given a weapon. Then he galloped until he joined the people, and he kept charging a man and killing him and crushing his spine. Sa'd looked at him in wonder and said: Who is this horseman? They remained only a little until Allah routed them. Then Abu Mihjan returned, returned the weapon, and put his feet in the shackles as before. Sa'd came, and his wife, or his umm walad, said to him: How was your fighting? He told her, saying: We met such and such, until Allah sent a man on a piebald horse; had I not left Abu Mihjan in shackles, I would have thought they were some of Abu Mihjan's traits. She said: By Allah, it is indeed Abu Mihjan; his affair was such and such. She told him the story. He said: So he summoned him, freed him from his bonds, and said: We shall never flog you for wine again. Abu Mihjan said: And I, by Allah, it shall never enter my head again; I only refused to leave it because of your flogging. He said: So he did not drink it after that."
"16560","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I was informed that Abu Ubaydah in Syria found Abu Jandal ibn Suhayl ibn Amr, Dirar ibn al-Khattab al-Muharibi, and Abu al-Azwar, and they were among the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), having drunk. Abu Jandal said: There is no blame upon those who believe and do righteous deeds for what they tasted, when they fear and believe and do righteous deeds, the verse. So Abu Ubaydah wrote to Umar: Abu Jandal disputed with me by this verse. Umar wrote: What made sin fair-seeming to Abu Jandal has made dispute fair-seeming to him; so subject them to hadd. Abu al-Azwar said: Will you subject us to hadd? Abu Ubaydah said: Yes. He said: Let us meet the enemy tomorrow; if we are killed, so be it, and if we return to you, then subject us to hadd. So Abu Jandal, Dirar, and Abu al-Azwar met the enemy; Abu al-Azwar was martyred and the other two were subjected to hadd. Abu Jandal said: I am ruined. Abu Ubaydah wrote of that to Umar, so he wrote to Abu Jandal, leaving Abu Ubaydah aside: What made sin fair-seeming to you has barred repentance from you: Ha Mim, the revelation of the Book from Allah, the Mighty, the Knowing, the Forgiver of sin and Accepter of repentance, severe in punishment, the verse."
"16561","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, who said: I heard Makhul saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever drinks wine, strike him. Then he said: Whoever drinks wine, strike him. Then he said the fourth time: Whoever drinks wine, kill him."
"16562","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, who said: I heard Amr ibn Shu'ayb narrating that Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him to Yemen, asked him, saying: My people make a drink from maize called mizr. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Does it intoxicate? He said: Yes. He said: Then forbid them from it. He said: I forbade them and they did not stop. He said: Then whoever does not stop the third time, kill him."
"16563","From Ma'mar, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When they drink, flog them, saying it three times; then he said: When they drink the fourth time, kill them. Ma'mar said: I mentioned that to Ibn al-Munkadir, and he said: Killing has been left; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought Ibn al-Nu'ayman and he flogged him, then he was brought and he flogged him, then he was brought and he flogged him, then he was brought the fourth time and he flogged him, or more."
"16564","From Ma'mar, from Zayd ibn Aslam, who said: Ibn al-Nu'ayman was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he flogged him; then he was brought and he flogged him, several times, four or five. A man said: O Allah, curse him, how often he drinks and how often he is flogged. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not curse him, for he loves Allah and His Messenger."
"16565","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When they drink, flog them; then when they drink, flog them; then when they drink, flog them; then when they drink, kill them. Then he said: Allah has lifted killing from them; when they drink, flog them; then when they drink, flog them, mentioning it four times."
"16566","From Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj, from al-Zuhri, from Qabisah ibn Dhu'ayb, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) flogged a man for wine three times, then he was brought the fourth time and he struck him also, adding nothing beyond that."
"16567","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever drinks wine, apply the hadd to him; if he drinks a second time, apply the hadd to him; if he drinks a third time, apply the hadd to him; if he drinks a fourth time, kill him. He said: Ibn al-Nu'ayman was brought having drunk, and he was struck with sandals and hands; then he was brought a second time and likewise; then a third time and likewise; then a fourth time and he applied the hadd to him, and lifted killing."
"16568","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, from Qabisah ibn Dhu'ayb, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck a man for wine four times. Then Umar ibn al-Khattab struck Abu Mihjan al-Thaqafi for wine eight times. As for Ibn Jurayj, he said: It reached me that Umar ibn al-Khattab flogged Abu Mihjan ibn Habib ibn Amr ibn Umayr al-Thaqafi for wine seven times."
"16569","From al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Abi al-Najud, from Dhakwan, from Mu'awiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Whoever drinks wine, flog him, saying it three times; then he said: If he drinks it four times, kill him."
"16570","From Ma'mar, from Zayd ibn Rufay', from Haram ibn Mu'awiyah, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to us: Let no pig neighbor you, let no cross be raised among you, do not eat at a table upon which wine is drunk, discipline horses, and walk between the two targets."
"16571","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah informed me, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad, freedman of Aslam, who informed him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for one who believes in Allah and the Last Day to sit at a table upon which wine is drunk, and it is not lawful for one who believes in Allah and the Last Day to stay away from Friday prayer. Abd al-Razzaq said: And I heard it from Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah with this isnad."
"16572","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Imran ibn Hudayr, who said: Abu Mijlaz prepared food and invited his companions to it. A man among them asked for drink, so drink was brought and he drank; then he began handing it to the one on his right. Abu Mijlaz said: Do not pass it around like a cup; leave it; whoever wishes to drink, let him call for it."
"16573","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: Does a woman comb her hair with intoxicant? He said: No. And Abd al-Karim said: No. And Amr ibn Dinar said: A woman does not comb with wine."
"16574","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: Aishah used to forbid a woman combing with intoxicant."
"16575","From Ma'mar, who said: Ikrimah was asked: Does a woman comb with intoxicant? He said: She does not comb in disobedience to Allah."
"16576","From Abdullah ibn Umar al-Madini, from Nafi', who said: It was said to Ibn Umar: Women comb with wine. Ibn Umar said: May Allah cast mange into their heads."
"16577","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Hudhayfah, who said: Women who comb with wine were mentioned, and he said: May Allah not make them fragrant."
"16578","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar found the smell of sausan in his house and said: Take it out; it is filth from the work of Satan."
"16579","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Abu Wa'il, from Abdullah: Allah did not place your cure in what He forbade you. From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, the like; Ma'mar said: And sakar is made from dates mixed with something."
"16580","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that Aishah used to forbid medication with wine."
"16581","From Abdullah, from Shu'bah, from Simak ibn Harb, from Alqamah ibn Wa'il al-Hadrami, from his father, that a man called Suwayd ibn Tariq asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about wine, and he forbade him from it. He said: I only make it for medicine. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is a disease, not a medicine. From Isra'il, from Simak, with this isnad, the same."
"16582","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, who said: Ibn Mas'ud said: Do not give your children wine to drink, for your children were born upon the fitrah. Would you give them to drink of what they have no knowledge of? Their sin is only upon whoever gave them to drink. Allah the Exalted did not place your cure in what He forbade you."
"16583","From Abdullah ibn Umar al-Madini, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that a slave of his gave a camel of his wine to drink, so he threatened him."
"16584","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, who said: He was told of a slave of his whose she-camel's leg had broken, and wine was prescribed for her. Ibn Umar said: Perhaps you gave her to drink. He said: No. He said: Had you done so, I would have hurt you with beating."
"16585","From al-Thawri, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, that Umar used to dislike treating the hind of his animal with wine."
"16586","Al-Thawri reported from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: They used to dislike giving their animals wine to drink, and rubbing with the dregs of wine. Al-Thawri said: One who is injected with wine breaks his fast, but the hadd is not applied to him; and if a man dyes with wine, there is no hadd upon him, but ta'zir."
"16587","From Ma'mar, from Sulayman al-Taymi, who said: A woman called Umm Hirash narrated to me that she saw Ali dyeing with wine-vinegar."
"16588","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from a woman called Umm Hirash, who said: I saw Ali take bread from a basket and dip it in wine-vinegar."
"16589","From Sa'id ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Tanukhi, from Atiyyah ibn Qays, who said: A man among Abu al-Darda's companions passed by, and a man was eating lunch and invited him to his food. He said: What is your food? He said: Bread, murri, and oil. He said: The murri that is made from wine? He said: Yes. He said: It is wine. So they agreed to go to Abu al-Darda, and they asked him. He said: Its wine has been slaughtered by the sun, salt, and fish, saying: There is no harm in it."
"16590","From Abd al-Quddus, that he heard Makhul saying: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: No vinegar from wine that was spoiled is lawful until Allah is the one who spoiled it. From Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah and others, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Ibn Shihab, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aslam, freedman of Umar, from Umar, the same. From Abd al-Wahhab, who said: I heard it from Ibn Abi Dhi'b."
"16591","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: Is wine made into vinegar? He said: Yes; and Amr ibn Dinar told me the same."
"16592","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, who said: I saw Ibn Sirin make wine-vinegar, or he said: sip wine-vinegar."
"16593","From Ma'mar, from Zayd ibn Rufay', from Ma'bad al-Juhani, who said: A man asked him about rubb made into nabidh, and he said: You revived it after it had died."
"16594","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aslam, freedman of Umar, who said: We came to al-Jabiyah with Umar, and we were brought tila, which is like thickened rubb, stirred only with a churn-stick. Umar said: In this drink is what it ends at."
"16595","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Amir ibn Shaqiq, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab provided them tila. A man asked him about tila, and he said: Umar used to provide us tila; we kneaded it into our sawiq, ate it with our condiment and bread; it is not your foul badhiq."
"16596","From Dawud ibn Ibrahim, who said: I asked Tawus about tila, and he said: There is no harm in it. I said: What is tila? He said: Have you seen something like honey that you eat with bread, and pour water upon it and drink it? Take it, and do not approach what is below it, nor drink it, nor give it to drink, nor sell it, nor seek help by its price."
"16597","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: It was written for Nuh two of everything, or he said: a pair; so he took what was written for him, and two grapevines strayed from him, and he set out seeking them. An angel met him and said to him: What do you seek? He said: Two grapevines. He said: Satan took them. The angel said: I shall bring you them and him. He said to him: He has a share in them, so make partnership with him well. He said: For me a third and for him two thirds. The angel said: You did well, and you are a doer of good; you may eat it as grapes and raisins, and cook it until two thirds go and a third remains. Ibn Sirin said: That agreed with the letter of Umar ibn al-Khattab."
"16598","From Ma'mar, from Asim, from al-Sha'bi, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab wrote to Ammar ibn Yasir: To proceed, drinks have come to us from Syria like camel-tila, cooked until the two thirds in which is Satan's filth, or he said: Satan's foulness, and the wind of his madness, have gone, and a third remains; so dip with it, and order those before you to dip with it."
"16599","From Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Suwayd ibn Ghaflah, who said: Umar wrote to his governors to provide people tila of which two thirds have gone and a third remains."
"16600","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, that Abu Talhah, Abu Ubaydah, and Mu'adh ibn Jabal used to drink tila when two thirds of it had gone and a third remained, meaning rubb."
"16601","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I heard Ata being asked about a woman whose leg, thigh, shin, or whatever of her breaks: may a male doctor who is not a mahram set it? He said: Yes, that is in necessity. Then Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr said: A woman dies with her child in her belly, and it is feared it will die; may a man cut into her and take her child from her belly? He said: That is not like anything else of her; had there been in that the healing there is in her best limb, it would be so. Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr said: When a she-camel breaks and it is feared for her, her child in her belly is cut out. But he refused it and disliked it for the woman."
"16602","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I heard Ata while a man asked him who was prescribed to stipulate upon his liver, then drink that blood, for a pain in him; so he allowed him in it. I said to him: Allah the Exalted forbade it. He said: Necessity. I said to him: Though he does not know there is healing in that. And I mentioned to him donkey milk at that, and he allowed it to be drunk as medicine."
"16603","From Ma'mar, from a man, from Jabir ibn Zayd, who said: A man used to treat women for fractures and the like, and Jabir said to him: Do not withhold any of that."
"16604","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, about a woman with a fracture or wound that only men can treat, who said: Allah the Exalted is more excusing with excuse."
"16605","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, who said: I heard him asked about cut ears being stitched: something intended for treatment."
"16606","From Ibn Abi Yahya, from a man he named, who said: Ali ibn al-Husayn drank donkey milk for an illness in him."
"16607","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, who said: I asked him about the milk of domestic donkeys, prescribed for his son, and he disliked it."
"16608","From al-Thawri, from Salim al-Aftas, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, who said: The meat of domestic donkeys and their milk were forbidden."
"16609","From al-Thawri, who said: They say: When the pregnant woman dies and it is hoped that what is in her belly may live, her belly is split. He said: It reached us that it lived. Al-Thawri said: And one of our companions said: He splits from the side of her left thigh."
"16610","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: People came to Madinah and found its climate heavy. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered camels for them and permitted them their urine and milk. When they recovered, they killed the herdsman and drove off the camels. They were brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he cut off their hands and feet, branded their eyes, and left them until they died. He said: And Hisham ibn Urwah said to me: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) branded their eyes. And he mentioned that Anas mentioned that to al-Hajjaj, whereupon al-Hasan said: Anas deliberately went to a devil and narrated to him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut and branded, faulting Anas for that. I said to him: What is branding? He said: A mirror or iron is heated, then brought near his eyes until they melt. From al-Thawri, perhaps from Ayyub, Abu Sa'id, doubting, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas, that they were from Ukl."
"16611","From Ma'mar, from Ata al-Khurasani: He saw no harm in being treated with urine."
"16612","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: A man of Banu Zuhrah informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In camel milk and urine is a cure for your dharab, meaning gall and the like of diseases."
"16613","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, who said: Whatever you eat its meat, drink its urine."
"16614","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, who said: Whatever you eat its meat, there is no harm in its urine. And Ibn Jurayj informed us from Ata the same."
"16615","From al-Thawri, from Aban ibn Abi Ayyash, from al-Hasan, who said: There is no harm in the urine of every cud-chewing animal."
"16616","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: There is no harm in camel urine; some of them would sniff it. He said: And they saw no harm in the urine of cattle and sheep."
"16617","From a man of Basra, from his father, from al-Hasan, that he allowed donkey urine for medicine."
"16618","From Isra'il ibn Yunus, from Majza'ah ibn Zahir, from his father, who had witnessed the Tree, that he complained, and it was prescribed for him to soak in donkey milk and its broth, meaning its meat cooked; so he disliked that."
"16619","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, that his father ordered a doctor to look at a wound in a woman's thigh, and he opened it for him, meaning he hollowed it for him."
"16620","Al-Thawri reported from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: Allah the Exalted sent down no disease except that He sent down with it a cure; so take cow milk, for it grazes from every tree."
"16621","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade what is between the two lava tracts of Madinah. Abu Hurayrah said: Had I found gazelles between its two lava tracts, I would not frighten them; and he made around Madinah twelve miles a sanctuary."
"16622","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr informed me that Rafi' ibn Khadij said while addressing in Madinah: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade what is between the two lava tracts of Madinah, or he said: it is it."
"16623","From Ma'mar, from Haram ibn Uthman, from Jabir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade every approaching herd coming upon Madinah from cutting trees, and something else he said; he said: except for one seeking a stray, or a stick for iron to benefit by."
"16624","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: It was narrated to me from Zayd ibn Thabit that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade hunting and thorn-trees between the two lava tracts of Madinah."
"16625","From Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out until, when he was at al-Suqya in the Harrah, he said: O Allah, Ibrahim, Your servant and Messenger, forbade Makkah; O Allah, I forbid what is between the two lava tracts of Madinah, as Ibrahim forbade Makkah."
"16626","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abd al-Karim ibn Abi al-Mukhariq informed me that Umar ibn al-Khattab said to the slave of Qudamah ibn Maz'un: You are over these woodcutters; whomever you find cutting wood between the two lava tracts of Madinah, his axe and rope are yours. He said: And his two garments? Umar said: No, that is too much."
"16627","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Ubaydullah ibn Umar informed me that Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas found a man cutting and beating down the thorn-trees at al-Aqiq, so he took his axe, his leather mat, and whatever else, and the slave went to his masters and told them the news. They went to Sa'd and said: The boy is our boy, so return to him what you took from him. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whomever you find cutting or gathering firewood of the thorn-trees of Madinah, a barid within a barid, his spoils are yours. I would not return anything the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave me."
"16628","From Ma'mar, from a man of Madinah, who said: Sa'd and Ibn Umar, when they found anyone cutting anything of the sanctuary, would strip him of his axe and rope."
"16629","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Ali, who said: We have nothing except the Book of Allah, except something in this sheet: Madinah is sacred, between Ayr and Thawr; whoever innovates in it, or shelters an innovator, upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels, and all people; Allah accepts from him neither sarf nor adl. Whoever takes as clients a people without permission of their patrons, upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels, and all people; Allah accepts from him neither sarf nor adl. The protection of Allah is one, sought by the lowest of them; whoever betrays a Muslim, upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels, and all people; neither adl nor sarf is accepted from him. And he says: Sarf and adl are voluntary and obligatory worship."
"16630","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yuhannas informed me, from Abu Abdullah al-Qarraz, that he said: I bear witness upon Abu Hurayrah that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever intends the people of this town with evil, meaning Madinah, Allah will melt him in the Fire as salt melts in water."
"16631","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Amr ibn Yahya ibn Umarah informed me that he heard Abu Abdullah al-Qarraz, of Abu Hurayrah's companions, claiming that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever intends its people with evil, Allah will melt him as salt melts in water."
"16632","From Abu Ma'shar, who said: I heard Abu Abdullah al-Qarraz saying: I heard Abu Hurayrah saying to Yazid ibn Mu'awiyah: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever intends the people of this town with evil, meaning Madinah, Allah the Exalted will melt him as salt melts in water."
"16633","From Ma'mar, from Zayd ibn Aslam, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, whoever intends Madinah with evil, melt him as lead melts in fire, as salt melts in water, and as fat melts in the sun."
"16634","From Muhammad ibn Abi Sabrah, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from Khalid ibn Yasar, from one of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever frightens the people of Madinah, Allah will frighten him."
"16635","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Hisham ibn Urwah informed me, from his father, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from Sufyan ibn Abi Zuhayr, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Yemen will be opened, and people will come driving and carrying their families and whoever obeys them, while Madinah is better for them had they known. Then Syria will be opened, and people will come driving and carrying their families and whoever obeys them, while Madinah is better for them had they known. Then Iraq will be opened, and people will come driving and carrying their families and whoever obeys them, while Madinah is better for them had they known."
"16636","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed me, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No one leaves Madinah out of dislike for it except that Allah replaces it with one better than him."
"16637","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from one of the people of knowledge, that he said: Whoever dies in Madinah, he will testify for him or intercede for him. From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), like the hadith of Ibn Jurayj."
"16638","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever endures the hardship and strain of Madinah, I shall be for him a witness or intercessor on the Day of Resurrection. He said: And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Faith will surely retreat to it as the flood draws back to the dung-heap."
"16639","From al-Thawri, from Ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, who said: A Bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and pledged to him upon Islam. Next day he came feverish and said: O Messenger of Allah, release me. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) refused. He came three consecutive days, each time saying: O Messenger of Allah, release me from my pledge, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) refused. When he turned away, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Madinah is like the forge; it expels its filth and brightens its good."
"16640","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Sa'id ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was commanded to a town that consumes towns. They say: Yathrib, and it is Madinah; it expels people as the forge expels filth."
"16641","From Yahya ibn al-Ala al-Bajali and others, from Ghalib ibn Ubaydullah, raising the hadith to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever visits me, meaning whoever comes to Madinah, shall be in my neighborhood; and whoever dies, meaning in one of the two sanctuaries, shall be raised among the secure on the Day of Resurrection."
"16642","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: It was narrated to me from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever says Yathrib for Madinah, let him say: I seek Allah's forgiveness, three times; it is Taybah, it is Taybah, it is Taybah. Ibn Uyaynah reported to us from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"16643","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed me, from his father, that Uhud appeared to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: This is a mountain that loves us and we love it."
"16644","From Ibn Abi Yahya, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, who said: I heard Anas ibn Malik saying: Uhud appeared to us while we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: This is a mountain that loves us and we love it."
"16645","From Ibn Abi Yahya, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Abu Layla, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Uhud is upon a channel of Paradise's channels, and the channel is a door and an opening upon a corner of the Fire's corners."
"16646","From Ibrahim ibn Abi Yahya, from Abdullah ibn Tammam, from a woman called Zaynab, from Anas ibn Malik, who said: Uhud is upon a door of Paradise's doors; when you come to it, eat of its trees, even of its thorn-trees."
"16647","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata, who said: Intent is the weapon; thus it reached us twice in succession."
"16648","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abd al-Karim narrated to us, from Ali and Ibn Mas'ud, that intent is the weapon."
"16649","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: I asked Tawus about Allah's saying concerning intent, what is it? He said: What do they say? I said: They say: The weapon. He said: Would Abu Abd al-Rahman say other than that? It is nothing but that. He said: And Ibn Tawus said to me: In what I informed you about the two women is a cure for the report of the two Hudhali women. He said: Were a man to come with a stone and crush a man's head with it, it is indeed intent."
"16650","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Amr ibn Shu'ayb said to me, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever kills deliberately, he is handed to the slain's family; if they wish they kill him, and if they wish they take the blood-money, a full Muslim blood-money, one hundred camels: thirty hiqqah, thirty jadha'ah, and forty khalifah; that is intent when its doer is not killed."
"16651","From Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah, from Amr ibn Sulaym, their freedman, from Ibn al-Musayyib, who said: Intent is iron, with a needle and above of weapons."
"16652","From one who heard al-Hasan saying: There is no intent except with iron."
"16653","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr, from al-Hasan, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no retaliation except with iron."
"16654","From Isra'il, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, from Masruq, who said: Intent is only with iron."
"16655","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, from Masruq, who said: Intent is only with iron."
"16656","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Abu Azib, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Everything besides the sword is mistake, and for every mistake is compensation."
"16657","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote: Whoever kills a believer by assault, it is retaliation, except that the slain's guardian is satisfied."
"16658","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: Deliberate killing among people, when they fight with swords, is retaliation between them; the Imam secures each slain and wounded his right, and if the slain's or wounded's guardian wishes, he retaliates, and if they settle upon blood-money, their settlement stands. And in the Sunna is that the Imam does not kill one whom the slain's guardians pardoned; the Imam is only a just arbiter between them, securing their rights upon them. Mistake is in play, throwing, hitting another, and the like; in it is blood-money, and blood-money is upon his aqilah in mistake. As for intent, then quasi-intent is upon him, unless the aqilah helps him, and upon them is to help him, as reached us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he said in the document he wrote between Quraysh and the Ansar: And do not leave a burdened one without helping him in ransom or blood-money."
"16659","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Ubayd ibn Umayr saying: The strong man goes and struts over a man with stick and stone until he splits his head, then says: It is not intent; and what intent is more intent than that?"
"16660","From Ma'mar, from Simak, that Urwah wrote to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz about a man who strangled a boy over some jewelry of his until he killed him, and they found the rope in his hand and he confessed to that; so he wrote: Hand him to the boy's guardians; if they wish they kill him."
"16661","From Ma'mar, from Simak, who said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote about a man who struck with a stone, saying: If he pushed him with the stone by thrusting, retaliate against him; and if he threw by throwing, do not retaliate against him."
"16662","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Qays, from al-Sha'bi, who said: When he repeats and begins with stick and stone, it is retaliation."
"16663","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, who said: When he repeats, meaning repeats: returns, it is retaliation."
"16664","From Isra'il, from Jabir, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, who said: Whoever strikes with a stick twice, in it is an aggravated blood-money. Jabir said: I asked al-Sha'bi and al-Hakam about a man who strikes two blows with a stick, then he dies; they said: An aggravated blood-money."
"16665","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Hakam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever kills a believer by assault, it is retaliation, except that the slain's guardian is satisfied; and the believers are all against him; it is not lawful for a believer who believes in Allah and the Last Day to shelter him and aid him; whoever shelters him and aids him, Allah's anger is upon him and He cursed him. And whatever you differ in of a thing, its judgment is to Allah."
"16666","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, about a man striking a man with a stick, who said: Quasi-intent; then if he repeats two and three times, in it is retaliation. And al-Hasan mentioned it from Mansur, from Ibrahim, the same."
"16667","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata, who said: Quasi-intent is stone and stick. He said that twice in succession. I said to him: Has other than that reached you? He said: We know not. I said to Ata: Stone and stick in less than life, by mistake, is quasi-intent? He said: Yes."
"16668","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: I said to Ata: If a man rises against a man with a stone and breaks his teeth, or with a stick and puts out his eye? He said: He is not retaliated against. Ibn Jurayj said: And I say: He is retaliated against, for that is not like life, that a man wounds a man not intending his life and it settles in his life; this one indeed intended his eye and teeth."
"16669","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, about a man who rises against a man with a stone, breaks his teeth and puts out his eye, who said: He is retaliated against."
"16670","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla informed me, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: Quasi-intent is stone, stick, whip, push, and shove, and everything you intended him with, in it is aggravation in blood-money. He said: And mistake is that he throws at a thing and misses with it."
"16671","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: What of push and shove you meet with? He said: That is not quasi-intent."
"16672","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abd al-Karim informed me, from Ali and Ibn Mas'ud, that quasi-intent is stone and stick."
"16673","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Quasi-intent is aggravated, and its doer is not killed; that is that Satan descends among people, so it is blind throwing without rancor and without bearing arms; whoever bears arms against us is not of us, nor one lying in wait on a road; whoever is killed upon other than this, it is quasi-intent, its blood-money is aggravated, and its doer is not killed."
"16674","Ibn Jurayj reported, saying: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that he heard Tawus saying: A man hit in throwing in fighting with a stick, whip, or one throwing with stones is compensated for, and he is not killed for him, because it is not known who his killer is. And I say: Do you not see to the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judgment concerning the two Hudhali women, when one struck the other with a tent-pole and killed her, that he did not kill her for her, but compensated for her and her fetus. Ibn Tawus reported it to us from his father."
"16675","Perhaps from Ibn Jurayj, Abu Sa'id said: Dropped from my book, he said: Ibn Tawus reported to us from his father, who said: With my father is a book in it mention of blood-moneys; revelation brought it to the Prophet: Whatever the Prophet judged of blood-money or charity, revelation brought it. He said: In that book, and it is from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Deliberate killing, its blood-money is the blood-money of mistake: stone, stick, and whip, so long as he bears no weapon."
"16676","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: Whoever is killed in an obscure communal killing, struck with a stone or a stick, then for him is an enhanced blood-money."
"16677","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is killed in a blind case, struck with a stone, or beaten with a whip, or with a stick, then his blood-money is the blood-money of mistake; and whoever is killed deliberately then it is retaliation, none shall come between him and his killer; so whoever comes between him and his killer, upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels, and all people; Allah accepts from him neither sarf nor adl."
"16678","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, who said: Quasi-intentional killing is a blow with bones, or with a stone, or the whip."
"16679","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, who said: Quasi-intentional killing is a blow with a thick piece of wood, and a huge stone."
"16680","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: Intentional killing is what is done with a weapon, and what is done without iron is quasi-intentional: the wood, the stone, and the stick; that he intends something but strikes another; and there is no quasi-intentional killing except concerning life."
"16681","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, who said: Mistake is that he shoots a man but strikes another, or shoots at something but misses with it."
"16682","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: Mistake is that you intend something but strike another."
"16683","From Ma'mar, who said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote concerning mistake: that he intends a man but strikes another."
"16684","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata, who said: The blood-money is enhanced in quasi-intentional killing, but there is no killing for it, twice in succession; from Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abd al-Karim reported to me, from Ali and Ibn Masud, like the saying of Ata."
"16685","From Ma'mar, from Ali ibn Zayd, from al-Qasim, from Ibn Umar, who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was on the steps of the Ka'bah, saying: Praise be to Allah who fulfilled His promise, aided His servant, and defeated the confederates alone; every pride of the Jahiliyyah is under my feet today, except the custodianship of the House and the watering of the pilgrims; between intentional and mistaken killing is killing with the whip and the stone, for them a hundred camels, forty of them pregnant with their young in their bellies."
"16686","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from al-Qasim ibn Rabi'ah, from Uqbah ibn Aws al-Sadusi, from a man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Makkah, he said: There is no god but Allah alone; He kept His promise, aided His servant, and defeated the confederates alone; every pride that is counted and claimed, and wealth and blood, are under these two feet, except the custodianship of the House and the watering of the pilgrims; the slain of mistake is the slain of the whip and the stick; al-Qasim said: forty of them pregnant with their young in their bellies; Khalid said: other than al-Qasim said: a hundred, forty of them pregnant with their young in their bellies."
"16687","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: The major blood-money which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enhanced is thirty hiqqah, thirty bint labun, and forty pregnant young fat she-camels."
"16688","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: Quasi-intentional killing is thirty hiqqah, thirty bint labun, and forty khalifah."
"16689","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, who said: In the book that is with my father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning quasi-intentional killing, like the hadith of Ma'mar; and he said to me: In that book, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): If they settle in intentional killing, then it is upon whatever they settled upon."
"16690","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, that Umar said: Concerning quasi-intentional killing, thirty jadha'ah, thirty hiqqah, and forty between thaniyyah and bazil of its year, all of them khalifah."
"16691","Ibn Jurayj reported from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever kills intentionally, then he is handed over to the family of the slain; if they wish they kill him, and if they wish they take the blood-money, a hundred camels: thirty hiqqah, thirty jadha'ah, and forty khalifah; that is the blood-money of intentional killing if its doer is not killed; from al-Thawri, from Mughirah and al-Shaybani, from al-Sha'bi, from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari and al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, like it."
"16692","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Salim and Sulayman al-Shaybani, from al-Sha'bi, from Zayd, who said: Concerning quasi-intentional killing, thirty hiqqah, thirty jadha'ah, and forty between thaniyyah and bazil of its year, all of them khalifah."
"16693","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: Forty khalifah, thirty hiqqah, and thirty jadha'ah."
"16694","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: Ali said: Concerning quasi-intentional killing, thirty-three hiqqah, thirty-three jadha'ah, and thirty-four between thaniyyah and bazil of its year, all of them khalifah."
"16695","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, that Ibn Masud said: Concerning quasi-intentional killing, twenty-five hiqqah, twenty-five jadha'ah, twenty-five bint makhad, and twenty-five bint labun; from al-Thawri, from Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Mijlaz, from Abu Ubaydah, like it."
"16696","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that Uthman and Zayd said: Concerning quasi-intentional killing, forty jadha'ah khalifah up to bazil of its year, thirty hiqqah, and thirty bint labun."
"16697","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, who said: If they settle in intentional killing upon something, then it is upon whatever they settled upon, whether less or more."
"16698","From Ma'mar, from a man, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, concerning the enhancing of cattle and sheep, who said: The quarter and the sixth."
"16699","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: I said to Ata: The enhancing of cattle and sheep? He said: I do not know it."
"16700","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Dawud ibn Abi Asim reported to me that the enhancing of cattle and sheep is the sixth; therein is no mention; he said: A fat thaniyyah is taken; I said to Dawud: Is what you report to me about the ages of cattle and sheep established? He said: It kept being done, and he does not attribute it to anyone he heard it from; he said: The people say it."
"16701","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Shihab said: The blood-money of mistake is five fifths: twenty of them bint labun, twenty bint makhad, twenty hiqqah, twenty jadha'ah, and twenty ibn labun."
"16702","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: Thirty hiqqah, thirty bint labun, thirty bint makhad, and ten male banu labun."
"16703","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: The blood-money of mistake from camels is thirty hiqqah, thirty bint labun, twenty bint makhad, and twenty male banu labun; from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, who said: In the book that is with my father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the blood-money of mistake, like the hadith of Ma'mar; from Ibn Jurayj, who said: From Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the blood-money of mistake, like it."
"16704","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: Ata said: The blood-money of mistake from camels is a hundred: twenty-five hiqqah, twenty-five jadha'ah, twenty-five bint makhad, and twenty-five male ibn labun."
"16705","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: Ali said: Concerning mistake there are twenty-five hiqqah, twenty-five jadha'ah, twenty-five bint makhad, and twenty-five bint labun."
"16706","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar reported to me that in a letter of Umar ibn al-Khattab, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The blood-money of the Muslim is a hundred camels in quarters; like this saying of Ali; and he added: If bint al-makhad is not found, a male banu labun is placed in its place."
"16707","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, that Ibn Masud said: Concerning intentional killing, in fifths: twenty hiqqah, twenty jadha'ah, twenty banat makhad, twenty ibn makhad, and twenty bint labun."
"16708","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, concerning the blood-money of mistake: thirty jadha'ah, thirty hiqqah, thirty bint labun, and ten male banu labun."
"16709","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: The blood-money from cattle is two hundred cows."
"16710","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, who said: The blood-money from cattle is two hundred cows; and Qatadah said: The thaniyyah and above is taken."
"16711","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has his blood-money in cattle, then two hundred cows; he said: And Abu Bakr said: Whoever has his blood-money in cattle, then for every camel two cows; and Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Upon the people of cattle are two hundred cows."
"16712","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Sha'bi, from Umar, who said: Upon the people of cattle are two hundred cows; Sufyan said: And we heard that it is musinnah."
"16713","From Ma'mar, from a man, from Makhul, who said: Two hundred cows; Ma'mar said: And Amr ibn Shu'ayb said: In mistake, the jadha' and the thani; and in the enhanced, the choicest of the property."
"16714","From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Sha'bi, concerning the ages of cattle, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Two hundred cows: a hundred jadha'ah and a hundred musinnah."
"16715","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Dawud ibn Abi Asim reported to me that the ages of cattle are a quarter tabi', a quarter whatever the clan assists with, of small or big or thani, and the rest from the middle of the property; he said: The people say it; Abd al-Razzaq said: Meaning whatever you wish of small or big."
"16716","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata, who said: The blood-money from sheep is two thousand sheep; from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, like it; and Qatadah said: The thaniyyah and above is taken, and no one-eyed, nor old, nor billy-goat is taken."
"16717","Ibn Jurayj reported from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has his blood-money from sheep, then two thousand sheep; and Abu Bakr said: Whoever has his blood-money from sheep, then for every camel twenty sheep; and Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Upon the people of sheep are two thousand sheep."
"16718","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Umar, who said: Upon the people of sheep are two thousand sheep."
"16719","From Ma'mar, from Ya'la, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, raising it to Umar ibn al-Khattab, who said: The thani and the jadha' are taken; as is taken in charity, so is taken in the blood-money of mistake."
"16720","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Dawud ibn Abi Asim reported to me that the ages of the blood-money of sheep are a quarter whatever crosses the valley, of small or big, and a quarter whatever the clan assists with, of small, big, and farid, and the rest from the middle of the property; therein is no mention; he said: He kept saying it, and the people say it."
"16721","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Shihab said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: The blood-money due in sheep is two thousand sheep."
"16722","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged for a life with the blood-money."
"16723","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: The blood-money in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was a hundred camels, for every camel an uqiyyah, so that is four thousand; then in the time of Umar camels became dear and silver cheap, so Umar made it an uqiyyah and a half; then camels became dear and silver cheap again, so Umar made it two uqiyyahs, so that is eight thousand; then camels kept becoming dear and silver cheap until he made it twelve thousand, or a thousand dinars; and from cattle two hundred cows, and from sheep a thousand sheep."
"16724","From Ibn Jurayj, that Ata said: The blood-money was from camels, until Umar ibn al-Khattab, when camels became dear, made it a hundred and twenty for every camel; he said: I said to Ata: If the villager wishes, may he give a hundred she-camels, or two hundred cows, or two thousand sheep, and not give gold? He said: If he wishes he gives camels and does not give gold; it is the first matter."
"16725","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: May the villager give, if he wishes, cows or sheep? He said: No; the people of the villages do not mutually pay from livestock except camels; he says: It is their blood-money in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"16726","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: It was said: Upon the people of camels are camels, upon the people of cattle are cattle, and upon the people of sheep are sheep."
"16727","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, who said: Upon the people of camels are camels, upon the people of gold is gold, upon the people of silver is silver, upon the people of sheep are sheep, and upon the people of cloth are garments."
"16728","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abd al-Karim reported to me, from al-Hasan, who said: If the owner of cattle or sheep wishes, he gives camels."
"16729","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: A hundred camels, or the value of that from other than it."
"16730","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made the blood-money a hundred of camels."
"16731","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Sha'bi, that Umar judged upon the people of silver ten thousand, upon dinars a thousand dinars, upon the people of garments two hundred garments, and upon the people of cattle two hundred cows; he said: And we heard that it is Sunnah; and upon the people of sheep two thousand sheep, and I heard that it is Sunnah; and upon the people of camels a hundred camels."
"16732","From Ma'mar, from a man, from Ikrimah, who said: Abu Bakr judged in place of every camel two cows; from Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Abu Bakr, like it."
"16733","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, who said: I heard Tawus saying: The blood-money of the Himyari is three hundred garments of the garments of al-Thalath."
"16734","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I asked Ata; he said: I said: The Bedouin owner of cattle and sheep, may he give camels if he wishes, even if the pursuer to whom blood-money is due dislikes it? He said: It is his right; he said: We see only that whatever the one to whom blood-money is due wishes is his right; for him is the livestock of the payer, whatever it is; it is not diverted to other than it if he wishes."
"16735","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: Ibn Tawus reported to us, from his father, that he used to say: Upon all people, villagers or Bedouins, is a hundred camels; whoever does not have camels, then upon the people of silver is silver, upon the people of cattle are cattle, upon the people of sheep are sheep, and upon the people of cloth is cloth; he said: They give of whatever kind it is, at the value of camels whatever it is, whether its value rises or falls on that day."
"16736","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: I said to Ibn Tawus: The people of grain, of dhurah, is food upon them? He said: I have not heard of that; Ibn Tawus said: His father said: Whoever meets with camels among the people, then camels are the right of the one to whom blood-money is due."
"16737","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Amr ibn Shu'ayb said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to value camels upon the people of the villages at four hundred dinars, or its equivalent in silver, and he valued them upon the prices of camels; when they became dear he raised their price, and when they became cheap he reduced their value upon the people of the villages, according to the price whatever it was; he said: And Abu Bakr judged concerning the blood-money upon the people of the villages when wealth multiplied and camels became dear, valuing a hundred camels at six hundred dinars to eight hundred; and Umar judged concerning the blood-money upon the people of the villages at twelve thousand, and said: I see the time in which the blood-money differs, falling in it from the value of camels and rising in it; and I see wealth has multiplied, and I fear for you the rulers after me, lest a Muslim man be struck and his blood-money be lost in falsehood, or his blood-money be raised without right and be loaded upon Muslim people and sweep them away; there is no increase upon the people of the villages in the enhancing of blood-money, nor in the sacred month, nor in the Sanctuary, nor is there enhancing upon the people of the villages in it; nothing is added in it beyond twelve thousand; and the blood-money of the people of the desert is upon the people of camels a hundred camels upon their ages, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged; and upon the people of cattle two hundred cows, and upon the people of sheep two thousand sheep; and were it valued upon the people of the villages except their blood-money in gold and silver, it would be valued upon them; and had the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged upon the people of the villages in gold and silver a named blood-money with no increase in it, we would have followed the judgment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it; but he used to value it upon the prices of camels."
"16738","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Yahya ibn Sa'id reported to me that Umar ibn al-Khattab fixed the blood-money from gold at a thousand dinars, and from silver at twelve thousand."
"16739","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar reported to me that in a letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, that Umar ibn al-Khattab consulted the predecessors when he enlisted the armies, and wrote: That upon the people of camels is a hundred camels, upon the people of cattle two hundred cows, upon the people of sheep two thousand sheep, and upon whoever weaves cloth of the people of Yemen the value of five hundred garments, or the value of that from other than garments; if the one struck is of the Bedouins, then his blood-money is from camels, the Bedouin is not burdened with gold or silver; and if the Bedouin strikes him, he pays him with a hundred camels; if he does not find camels, then its equivalent from sheep, two thousand sheep; and Uthman judged in the enhancing the blood-money at four thousand dirhams."
"16740","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ikrimah, who said: A freedman of Banu Adi ibn Ka'b killed a man of the Ansar, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged in his blood-money twelve thousand dirhams; and he said: And it is he who says: And they resented only that Allah and His Messenger enriched them of His bounty."
"16741","From Ma'mar, from a man, from Ikrimah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: There is no enhancing upon the people of the villages, because gold is upon them, and gold is enhancing; from Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from Umar, like it."
"16742","From Ma'mar, from a man, from Ikrimah, who said: The blood-money is enhanced only in the ages of camels, not in gold nor in silver; gold and silver are themselves enhancing."
"16743","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, who said: Uthman judged in the enhancing of the blood-money at four thousand dirhams."
"16744","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he used to enhance in the blood-money of the neighbor, and of one killed in the sacred month."
"16745","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, and from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, who said: Whoever is killed in the sacred month, whoever is killed while in ihram, and whoever is killed in the Sanctuary, then the blood-money and a third of the blood-money; from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, like it."
"16746","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, who said: Whoever is killed in the Sanctuary, then the blood-money and a third of the blood-money; and whoever is killed in ihram, then the blood-money is enhanced."
"16747","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from his father, who said: A man made a mare trample a woman in the season, breaking a rib of her ribs, and she died, so Uthman judged in her eight thousand dirhams, because it was in the Sanctuary, making it the blood-money and a third of the blood-money; from al-Thawri and Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from his father, like it, except that Ibn Uyaynah said: In Makkah in Dhu al-Qa'dah."
"16748","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: Ata said to me concerning a man who kills his neighbor: Therein is enhancing, they claimed; I said: What of a relative? He said: It has reached us that therein is enhancing; I said: The son of a paternal aunt? He said: Yes; in every relative there is enhancing."
"16749","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata, who said: He said to me: Enhancing in the sacred month, and in the Sanctuary."
"16750","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: Amr ibn Dinar and Sulayman al-Ahwal reported to me that they heard Tawus saying: In the Sanctuary, in the neighbor, and in the sacred month there is enhancing; from Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr, from Ibn Tawus, like it, and he added in it, he said: Enhancing in the ages of camels, and nothing is added in the blood-money."
"16751","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: Ibn Tawus reported to me, from his father, that he used to say, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Concerning the neighbor and the sacred month there is enhancing."
"16752","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: Ibn Tawus reported to me, from his father, who said: I asked Ibn Abbas, or a man asked him, about a man who killed a neighbor of his in the sacred month and in the Sanctuary, so Ibn Abbas said: I do not know; and Ibn Tawus used to say nothing in it."
"16753","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: Ata said: If one in halal kills one in ihram, his blood-money is enhanced; and if one in ihram kills one in halal, it is enhanced in his blood-money."
"16754","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: Concerning wounds there is enhancing in the sacred month."
"16755","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: Ibn Abi Najih reported to me, from Mujahid, that he said: Concerning the face of a woman there is enhancing; and that he said: Concerning the lower lip there is enhancing in it, of a man and a woman; and he used to say: Enhancing is not an increase in the number of the property, but in the preferring of camels; every two whose measure is equal, then one of them is preferred, then it is enhancing, and not an increase in the number of the property."
"16756","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Amr ibn Shu'ayb reported to me, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: There is no increase upon the people of the villages in the enhancing of blood-money, nor in the sacred month, nor in the Sanctuary."
"16757","From Ma'mar, from Layth, from Mujahid, that Umar ibn al-Khattab judged concerning one killed in the sacred month, or in the Sanctuary, or while in ihram, with the blood-money and a third of the blood-money."
"16758","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, and Ash'ath, from al-Sha'bi, they agreed that there is no enhancing in the Sanctuary, nor in the one in ihram, nor in the like of that."
"16759","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, Sulayman ibn Yasar, and Ata ibn Abi Rabah, who said: Whoever is killed in the sacred month, then a blood-money and a third; Qatadah said: I mentioned that to al-Hasan, and he said: I do not know this."
"16760","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: Whatever is struck of the livestock and properties of people in the sacred month, then the third is added; this is in the intentional."
"16761","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Aban ibn Uthman, that Uthman fined concerning a she-camel of one in ihram destroyed by a man, fining him the third in addition to its price."
"16762","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Aban ibn Uthman, who said: Uthman was brought a man who had taken into his care the stray of a man in the sacred month, and it was lost with him, so he fined him its price and the like of a third of its price."
"16763","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: Amr ibn Muslim reported to me, from Tawus and Ikrimah, that he heard them saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Concerning the concealed stray of camels: its ransom is its like if he delivers it after concealing it, or it is found with him, then upon him is its mate, its like."
"16764","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: The deaf month of Allah is Rajab; he said: And the Muslims used to magnify the sacred months, because wrongdoing in them is graver; he said: And whoever is killed in a halal month, or wounded, is not killed for in a sacred month, until a halal month comes; Allah the Exalted said: The sacred month for the sacred month."
"16765","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata, who said: He reported to me that a man was wounded in a halal month, and Uthman ibn Muhammad, while a commander, wanted to retaliate for him in a sacred month, so Ubayd ibn Umayr, while in a company at the house, sent to him: Do not retaliate for him until a halal month enters."
"16766","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: I said to Ata: Do you see a man killed in the Sanctuary, where is his killer killed? He said: Wherever the family of the slain wish; he said: And if he killed in the hill he is not killed in the Sanctuary; and likewise the sacred months are like the Sanctuary in that; from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, like it."
"16767","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: Whoever kills in the Sanctuary is killed in the Sanctuary, and whoever is killed in the hill then enters the Sanctuary is brought out to the hill and killed; he said: That is the Sunnah."
"16768","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, who said: Whoever kills or steals in the hill, then enters the Sanctuary, then he is not sat with, nor spoken to, nor sheltered, and is adjured until he comes out, then it is carried out upon him; and whoever kills or steals and is seized in the hill then brought into the Sanctuary, and they want to carry out upon him what he did, he is brought out from the Sanctuary to the hill; and if he killed in the Sanctuary or stole, it is carried out upon him in the Sanctuary."
"16769","Ibn Uyaynah reported, from Ibn Tawus and Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, concerning one who killed in the hill then entered the Sanctuary, who said: He is not sat with, nor spoken to, nor traded with, nor sheltered; Ibn Tawus said: And he is reminded; and Ibrahim said: He is come to and it is said: O so-and-so, fear Allah concerning the blood of so-and-so; come out of the inviolable places."
"16770","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, and Mutarrif, from al-Sha'bi, who said: If he kills in the Sanctuary or commits a hadd in the Sanctuary, it is carried out upon him in the Sanctuary; and if he kills in other than the Sanctuary then enters the Sanctuary, he is safe."
"16771","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: Ibn Abbas blamed Ibn al-Zubayr concerning a man he seized in the hill, then brought him into the Sanctuary, then brought him out to the hill and killed him, saying: He brought him into the Sanctuary, then brought him out to the hill and killed him; meaning he says: He brought him in with safety, then brought him out; and that man Ibn al-Zubayr had accused in some matter, and he aided Abd al-Malik against him; and Ibn Abbas did not see killing upon him; and Ibn al-Zubayr did not remain after him but a little until he was killed."
"16772","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata, who said: Concerning the mudiha, five camels."
"16773","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul, from Qabisah ibn Dhuayb, from Zayd ibn Thabit, who said: Concerning the mudiha, five camels."
"16774","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged concerning the mudiha with five camels, or their equivalent in gold, silver, cattle, or sheep."
"16775","From Ibn Jurayj and Ma'mar, who said: Ibn Tawus reported to us, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Concerning the mudiha, five."
"16776","From Ma'mar, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from his father, from his grandfather: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged concerning the mudiha with five camels."
"16777","From al-Thawri and Muhammad, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, who said: Concerning the mudiha, five camels."
"16778","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from some of their companions, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not judge in less than the mudiha with anything."
"16779","Ibn Jurayj reported from Sulayman ibn Musa, who said: Umar wrote to the armies: And we do not know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged in less than the mudiha with anything; he said: And Umar ibn al-Khattab judged concerning the mudiha with five camels, or their equivalent in gold or silver; and concerning the mudiha of a woman with five camels, or their equivalent in gold or silver."
"16780","From Ma'mar, who said: A man of the people of Yemen reported to me that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: The mudiha is measured by the thumb; whatever exceeds that is taken by account of what exceeds."
"16781","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, who said: Whatever is less than the mudiha is government assessment."
"16782","From Ismail ibn Abdullah Abu al-Walid, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not judge in less than the mudiha with anything."
"16783","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul, from Qabisah ibn Dhuayb, from Zayd ibn Thabit, who said: Concerning the damiyah, a camel; concerning the badi'ah, two camels; concerning the mutalahimah, three camels; concerning the simhaq, four; concerning the mudiha, five; concerning the hashimah, ten; concerning the manqulah, fifteen; concerning the ma'mumah, a third of the blood-money; concerning a man beaten until his intellect departs, the full blood-money; or beaten until he moans and does not understand, the full blood-money; or becomes hoarse and does not understand, the full blood-money; concerning the eyelid, a quarter of the blood-money; and concerning the nipple of the breast, a quarter of the blood-money."
"16784","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: Ibn Abi Mulaykah reported to me: Concerning two men who disputed to Abdullah ibn Khalid about a mudiha, and he said: Therein is nothing; so I mentioned that to Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, and he said: Abdullah ibn Khalid spoke truly; Umar ibn al-Khattab used to say: Concerning the mudiha, the people of the villages do not pay it, but the people of the desert pay it."
"16785","From Ibn Jurayj, that Umar ibn al-Khattab used to say: The mudiha is only upon the people of the deserts; he said: As for upon the people of the villages, then no; he said: I have lived and the people of the villages did not mutually pay it."
"16786","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: I heard Ibn Abi Mulaykah saying: Umayr ibn Khalid, freedman of Amr ibn al-As, came to Ibn al-Zubayr seeking a mudiha he was struck with, I reckoned for him; so Ibn al-Zubayr said: Therein is nothing; Ibn al-Zubayr said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: The people of the villages do not pay it, but the people of the desert pay it."
"16787","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Amr ibn Dinar reported to me, from Abdullah ibn Safwan, from Amir al-Ghifari, that Umar ibn al-Khattab annulled the mudiha for the people of the villages."
"16788","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: I heard Abd al-Aziz narrating, from Abu Salamah ibn Sufyan, that Umar ibn al-Khattab annulled it for the people of the villages."
"16789","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: I said to Ata: Is the mudiha upon the people of the desert five? He said: Yes."
"16790","From Ma'mar, who said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to the people of the villages to pay the mudiha, and made in it fifty dinars."
"16791","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: I said to Ata: A mudiha in other than the head, in the face, or in the hand, do the people of the desert pay it? He said: Yes by Allah; I think it when it exposes the bone."
"16792","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab judged concerning the mudiha that is in the body of a man, not in his head, judging that every bone that has a named due, then in its mudiha is half a tenth of its due whatever it is; so if the mudiha is in the hand, then it is half a tenth of its due, so long as it is not in the fingers; so if there is a mudiha in the fingers, then it is half a tenth of it; and that is because the fingers have separate dues, every finger being ten camels; and whatever is above the fingers of the palm, then its due is like the due of the forearm and the upper arm; and he judged concerning the leg like what he judged concerning the hand of the due, in its fingers and its mudiha."
"16793","From Ma'mar, from a man, from Ikrimah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab judged concerning the mudiha of a finger half a tenth of the due of that finger."
"16794","Ibn Jurayj reported, who said: Yahya ibn Sa'id reported to me, who said: I heard Sulayman ibn Yasar mentioning that the mudiha in the face is like the mudiha in the head, except that there is a defect in the face, then it is added in the mudiha of the face by the measure of the defect of the face, between it and the due of half the mudiha."
"16795","I heard al-Hajjaj ibn Artah narrating, from Makhul, from Zayd ibn Thabit, who said: Concerning the mudiha that is in the head, the eyebrow, and the nose, alike."
"16796","From Ma'mar, from Ata al-Khurasani, who said: If the mudiha is in the body of a man, then therein are twenty-five dinars; and if it is in the hand, then like that."
"16797","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: The wounds of the head and the face are alike."
"16798","Ma'mar reported from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz said: Concerning the mudiha in the face and the head, alike."
"16799","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, who said: They are alike; he said: And there is no mudiha of the body, therein is only government assessment."
"16800","From Ma'mar, from a man, from Ibn al-Musayyib, who said: Concerning the mudiha in the face is double what is in the mudiha of the head."
"16801","From Ma'mar, from a man, from Ikrimah freedman of Ibn Abbas, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab judged concerning the wounds about which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not judge, nor Abu Bakr, judging concerning the mudiha that is in the body of a man and not in the head, that every bone that has a named due, then in its mudiha is half a tenth of its due whatever it is; so if there is a mudiha in the hand, then half a tenth of its due, so long as it is not in the fingers, then it is half a tenth of the due of the finger; and whatever is above the fingers in the palm, then its due is like the mudiha in the forearm and the upper arm; and in the leg is like what is in the hand."
"16802","From al-Thawri, from Jabir ibn Abdullah ibn Nujayy, that Ali judged concerning the simhaq, which is the multa'ah, with four camels; from al-Thawri, from Mansur, from al-Hakam, from Ali, like it."
"16803","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul, from Qabisah ibn Dhuayb, from Zayd ibn Thabit, who said: Concerning the damiyah, a camel; concerning the badi'ah, two camels; concerning the mutalahimah, three; concerning the simhaq, four; and concerning the mudiha, five; from Ma'mar, from Qatadah, like it; Ma'mar said: And I know not but he mentioned it from Ali; from Ibn Jurayj, who said: I was told, from Abd al-Malik, like the saying of Zayd, except that he did not mention the mudiha."
"16804","I said to Malik: Al-Thawri reported to us from you, from Yazid ibn Qusayt, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that Umar and Uthman judged concerning the multa'ah with half the mudiha; so he said to me: I did narrate it to him; so I said: Then narrate it to me; but he refused, and said: The practice with us is upon other than that, and the man with us is not there; meaning Yazid ibn Qusayt."
"16805","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I was told from al-Sha'bi, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that he said: Concerning the greater damiyah, they see it is the mutalahimah, three hundred dirhams; concerning the mudiha, two hundred dirhams; and concerning the lesser damiyah, a hundred dirhams."
"16806","I heard a freedman of Sulayman ibn Habib narrating, informing Ma'mar, that Sulayman ibn Habib judged concerning the sakkah, when it reddens or greens or blackens, with six dinars."
"16807","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul, from Qabisah ibn Dhuayb, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that he said: Concerning the hashimah, ten camels."
"16808","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, who said: Therein are ten camels; Qatadah said: And some of them said: Seventy-five dinars."
"16809","From al-Thawri, who said: Concerning the hashimah in the head, we heard that therein are a thousand dirhams."
"16810","From Ma'mar, who said: It reached me that concerning the harsah, forty-five dirhams."
"16811","From Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from al-Sha'bi, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that he said: Concerning the harsah that is between the flesh and the skin in the head, fifty dirhams."
"16812","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, who said: Concerning the mudiha of the slave and his tooth, in each of them half a tenth of his price."
"16813","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Zakariyya, from al-Sha'bi, concerning the mudiha of the slave, half a tenth of his price."
"16814","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata, who said: Concerning the ma'mumah, the third."
"16815","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, who said: Concerning the ma'mumah, a third of the blood-money; from al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim, from Ali, like it."
"16816","From Ma'mar, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged concerning the ma'mumah a third of the blood-money."
"16817","From Ibn Jurayj, Ma'mar, and al-Thawri, all from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, concerning the ma'mumah a third of the blood-money; and if it deranges his side, or he faints from thunder, or his intellect departs, then therein is the full blood-money."
"16818","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Abi Najih reported to me that Mujahid used to say: Concerning three of the ma'mumah is the blood-money: if it deranges his side, or his intellect departs, or he faints from thunder."
"16819","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Tawus reported to me, who said: With my father is a book, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Concerning the ma'mumah, thirty-three."
"16820","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul, from Qabisah ibn Dhuayb, from Zayd ibn Thabit, who said: Concerning the ma'mumah, a third of the blood-money; Muhammad said: And I heard Makhul saying: If the ma'mumah is intentional, then therein are two thirds of the blood-money; and if it is a mistake, then therein are two thirds of the blood-money."
"16821","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: For the mamumah is one third of the blood money, thirty-three camels, or its value in gold or silver. He said: And Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled likewise. He said: And Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled: For a mamumah on the body, if the shin, thigh, forearm or upper arm is struck until its marrow comes out and its bone bends and does not set, therein is half of the head mamumah, sixteen young she-camels and a half."
"16822","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, he said: For the munaqqilah is fifteen."
"16823","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul, from Qabisah ibn Dhuayb, from Zayd ibn Thabit, he said: For the munaqqilah is fifteen."
"16824","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ata said to me: For the munaqqilah is fifteen camels. He said: And Ibn Abi Mulaykah said it also."
"16825","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, he said: In the book with my father, and it is from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: For the munaqqilah is fifteen. From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, the same."
"16826","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: For the manqulah is fifteen camels, or its value in gold, silver or sheep. And Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled likewise for the manqulah of a man and a woman."
"16827","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled that any manqulah whose bones are displaced in the upper arm, forearm, shin or thigh is half of the head manqulah, seven young she-camels and a half."
"16828","From Mamar, from a man, from Ikrimah, from Umar: For a body manqulah is half of the head manqulah, when its bones are displaced in the forearm, upper arm, shin or thigh."
"16829","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Ata, he said: I said to him: For shaving of the head, is there a fixed compensation. He said: I do not know."
"16830","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani informed me, he said: Ibn Sirin said to me: If some of your beard were plucked, what would be due in that. Then Muhammad said: Shurayh said: It is placed on the scale, and if what remains does not make up in the beard, then from the head hair. Sufyan said: We heard that if the head is shaved and does not grow, or the beard, then for each of them is the full blood money."
"16831","From Israil, from al-Minhal ibn Khalifah, from Tamim ibn Salamah, he said: A man poured a pot over the head of a man, and his hair went away, so he went to Ali, and he ruled against him the full blood money."
"16832","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, concerning a man who plucked from the beard of a man, he said: Retaliation is taken from him by weight, and what does not suffice is completed from the head hair."
"16833","Sufyan said: We heard that for the forehead, when broken, is a judicial assessment."
"16834","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, he said: For the forehead, when crushed with an inward depression, one hundred and fifty dinars. If between the eyebrows there is a break disfiguring the face, and no bones moved from it, then a quarter of the blood money. If what is between the ears is broken, reaching the chewing muscles of the jaws, with hair covering it, in a depression not struck by the wound, and no bone moved from it, then therein is one hundred dinars."
"16835","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I said to Ata: For the eyebrow that is cut off. He said: I have not heard anything about it."
"16836","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: For the two eyebrows is the full blood money, and for one of them half the blood money. From Mamar, from Qatadah, the same as the saying of Ibn al-Musayyib, and he added: Whatever is lost of the eyebrow is by that reckoning."
"16837","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from al-Shabi, he said: For the two eyebrows is the full blood money. He said: And another said: A fair judicial assessment."
"16838","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: Abu Bakr ruled: For the eyebrow, when injured until its hair is gone, he ruled therein two mudihahs, ten camels."
"16839","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, it reached him from the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: For the eyebrow whose hair falls out, therein is a quarter, and for what is lost of it by reckoning. If the eyebrow is struck with a wound laying bone bare and its hair removed, then it is only the compensation of the eyebrow, with no compensation for the mudihah. If struck with a munaqqilah, then it is the compensation of the eyebrow and the munaqqilah together."
"16840","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, he said: It was agreed for Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz concerning the upper eyelid, when plucked, half the blood money of the eye, and for the lower eyelid a third of the blood money of the eye. And they said: If the eyelid is gone so the eye becomes sightless, then the ransom of the eye."
"16841","From Mamar, from one of his companions, from al-Shabi, he said: For every eyelid is a quarter of the blood money of the eye."
"16842","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Makhul, from Qabisah ibn Dhuayb, from Zayd ibn Thabit, he said: For the eyelid is a quarter of the blood money."
"16843","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: For the ear, when uprooted, fifty camels. From Ibn Jurayj and Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, the same."
"16844","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, he said: For the ear, then the half, meaning half the blood money. Sufyan said: Whatever is injured of the ear is by that reckoning."
"16845","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: For the ear, when uprooted, half the blood money, and when hearing is gone, then half the blood money."
"16846","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, that he said: Abu Bakr said: For the ear, fifteen camels, which hair and turban conceal."
"16847","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The first who ruled concerning the ear was Abu Bakr, fifteen camels, harming no hearing and reducing no strength, concealed by hair and turban."
"16848","From Ibn Jurayj, that Alqamah ibn Qays said: Ibn Masud said: For every pair is the blood money, and for every single is the blood money."
"16849","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, that Abu Bakr ruled for the ear fifteen camels, and said: It is only a thing harming no hearing and reducing no strength, concealed by hair and turban."
"16850","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled for the ear, when uprooted, half the blood money. From Mamar, from Amr ibn Muslim, from Tawus and Ikrimah, that Umar ruled by it. Mamar said: And the people follow it."
"16851","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: For the ear, when uprooted, half the blood money, and what is cut of it is by that reckoning, measured with paper. Qatadah said: And when hearing is gone, then half its blood money. He said: And Abu Bakr ruled therein fifteen camels."
"16852","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: If its hearing goes but it is not cut, its compensation is complete. If it is cut and its hearing goes, therein is the full blood money, one thousand dinars."
"16853","From Mamar, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: Abu Bakr ruled concerning the ear and made it a manqulah. He said: Its hearing does not go, and hair and turban cover it. And Umar ruled therein half the blood money, or its value in gold or silver."
"16854","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: If the ear is cut, its compensation is complete. He said: And Abu Bakr ruled therein fifteen camels."
"16855","From Humayd al-Shami, from al-Hajjaj, from Makhul, from Zayd, he said: For the earlobe is a third of the blood money."
"16856","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Nothing has reached me about loss of hearing."
"16857","From Ibn Jurayj, from Umar, he said: I asked Ibn Ulathah, I said: A man claims he deafened him by his blow, how is that known. He said: His unguarded moments are sought, and if anything is established, otherwise he is made to swear, then he is given. If he claims deafness in one ear and not the other, the one not deafened is stuffed, and his unguarded moments are sought."
"16858","From al-Thawri, he said: It reached me from Ibrahim and others, he said: He is taken unawares, and it is seen whether he hears or not."
"16859","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: For loss of hearing is fifty."
"16860","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It reached me that a man came to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz and said: So-and-so struck me until one of my ears became deaf. He said: He said to him: How do we know that. He said: Call the physicians. So he called them, and they made him smell, and they said of the deaf one: This is the deaf one."
"16861","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: What was agreed for Umar is that he said: I have not heard about anything injured that he covers his mouth and nostrils, and if he hears a whistling in the ear when covered, then there is no harm in him."
"16862","From Mamar, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, wrote for them a letter: And for the eye is fifty camels."
"16863","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, he said: For the eye is half the blood money. From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, the same."
"16864","From Ibrahim ibn Tahman, from Ashath ibn Sawwar, from al-Shabi, that Ibn Masud said: The two eyes are equal."
"16865","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, they said: For the two eyes is the full blood money, and for the eye half the blood money, and what is lost is by that reckoning. It was said to Mamar: And how is that known. He said: It reached me from Ali that he said: He closes his injured eye, then looks with the other, and sees where his sight ends, then looks with the injured one, and what is deficient is by its reckoning."
"16866","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ata said to me: For the eye is fifty. He said: I said to Ata: Then some of its sight is gone and some remains. He said: By reckoning of what is gone. He holds over the sound one and looks with the other, then holds over the other and looks with the sound, and reckons what is gone of it."
"16867","From Mamar, from a man, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, he said: A man slapped a man, or other than a slap, except that his sight went while his eye stands, and they wanted to restrain him, but it baffled them and the people how to restrain him, and they did not know what to do. Then Ali came to them and ordered him, and placed cotton on his face, then faced him to the sun, and brought a mirror near his eye, and his sight gleamed while his eye stands."
"16868","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: It reached me, he said: I think it is from Ali, that he said: He closes his injured eye, then looks with the other, and sees where his sight ends, then looks with this injured one, and what is deficient is taken by its reckoning."
"16869","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I said to Ata: His eye weakened from old age, then was injured. He said: Its compensation is full. And he said about a sick man killed: His blood money is full. And Abd al-Karim said the same."
"16870","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, he said: In the book with my father, and it is from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: For the eye is fifty."
"16871","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: For the eye is half the blood money, fifty camels, or its value in gold, silver, sheep or cattle."
"16872","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: For the eye is half the blood money, or its value in gold or silver. And for the eye of a woman is half her blood money, or its value in gold or silver."
"16873","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who gouged the eye of a man, then the cousin of the victim stood and killed him, he said: The blood money of the eye is placed in the property of the slain, because it was deliberate, and the killer is subjected to retaliation for the one he killed."
"16874","From Mamar, from Ibn Shubrumah, concerning a man who gouged the eye of a man, then became blind, he said: If he was brought to the ruler and he ruled retaliation against him, he pays it. If he became blind before he rules, then he has nothing. Likewise the killer who dies or is killed after judgment against him, he pays."
"16875","From Mamar, from Qatadah, that a man gouged his own eye, and Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled for him its blood money upon his clan."
"16876","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, they said: If the eye of the one-eyed is gouged, the eye of the one who gouged it is gouged, and he also pays the one-eyed five hundred dinars. If the eye of the one-eyed is gouged by mistake, then for it is the blood money, one thousand dinars."
"16877","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me that the one-eyed whose eye is gouged, therein is the full blood money. I said: From whom. He said: We have always heard it. He said: And Rabiah said that."
"16878","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, they said: If the one-eyed gouges the eye of a sound man deliberately, he pays one thousand dinars. If he gouges it by mistake, he pays five hundred dinars."
"16879","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man with whiteness in one eye, whose sound eye was injured, he said: We deem that added to the blood money of his eye what was lacking of the other uninjured one."
"16880","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told from Ibn al-Musayyib that Umar and Uthman ruled for the eye of the one-eyed the full blood money."
"16881","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Iyad informed me that Umar and Uthman agreed that for the eye of the one-eyed is the full blood money."
"16882","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ayyub ibn Musa informed me that Raja ibn Haywah informed him that the chief of the guard of Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan, his whip struck the eye of a one-eyed man and gouged it. He said: Abd al-Malik gave him therein one thousand dinars."
"16883","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Umar ibn al-Khattab: For the eye, when nothing of his sight remains except it, the full blood money. And for the eye of a woman, when nothing of her sight remains except it, then injured, the full blood money."
"16884","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from Abu Mijlaz, from Abd Allah ibn Safwan, that Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled for the eye of a one-eyed, whose sound eye was gouged, the full blood money."
"16885","From Said, from Qatadah, from Khilas ibn Amr, from Ali: Concerning a one-eyed man whose sound eye was gouged deliberately, if he wishes he takes the full blood money, and if he wishes he gouges an eye and takes half the blood money."
"16886","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, concerning the eye of the one-eyed injured, he said: Half the blood money."
"16887","From al-Thawri, from Firas, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, concerning the eye of the one-eyed injured, he said: I pay, O slain of Allah, therein is the half."
"16888","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Abu al-Duha, he said: Abd Allah ibn Maqil was asked about a man gouging the eye of the one-eyed, he said: I did not gouge his other eye, therein is the half."
"16889","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from some Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: For the eye of the one-eyed is fifty camels."
"16890","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: I said to Ata: The one-eyed injures the eye of a man deliberately, is retaliation taken from him. He said: I do not deem retaliation taken from him, I deem for him the full blood money."
"16891","From Uthman, from Said, from Qatadah, from Abu Iyad, that Uthman ruled concerning a one-eyed man who gouged the eye of a sound man, he said: Upon him is the blood money of his eye, and no retaliation upon him. Qatadah said: And Ibn al-Musayyib said: No retaliation is sought from the one-eyed, and upon him is the full blood money, if deliberate."
"16892","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, they said: If the one-eyed gouges the eye of the sound deliberately, he pays one thousand dinars. If he gouges it by mistake, he pays five hundred dinars."
"16893","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Muhammad ibn Abi Iyad, that Umar and Uthman agreed that the one-eyed, if he gouges the eye of another, upon him is like the blood money of his eye. And he mentioned that Ali said: Allah established retaliation in His Book: And the eye for the eye, and He knew this, so upon him is retaliation, for Allah was not forgetful."
"16894","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled for the standing eye, when gouged, a third of its blood money."
"16895","Mamar said: And it reached me that Qatadah said: From Abd Allah ibn Buraydah, from Yahya ibn Yamar, from Ibn Abbas, that Umar ruled for the paralyzed hand, the standing blind eye, and the black tooth, for each of them a third of its blood money. From Uthman ibn Matar, from Said, from Qatadah, from Abd Allah ibn Buraydah, from Yahya ibn Yamar, from Ibn Abbas, the same."
"16896","Al-Thawri reported, he said: Yahya ibn Said informed me, from Bukayr ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, that Zayd ibn Thabit ruled for the standing eye, when poked, one hundred dinars."
"16897","Ibn Jurayj and Mamar reported, they said: Ibn Abi Najih informed us, from Mujahid, that for the standing eye that does not see, if pierced or poked, therein was half the compensation of the eye, twenty-five, and if he had already taken its compensation the first time."
"16898","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Dawud ibn Abi Asim, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, that Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled for the standing eye poked a third of its blood money."
"16899","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, for the standing eye poked a third of its blood money."
"16900","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ismail ibn Umayyah informed me that Bukayr ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj informed him that he heard Sulayman ibn Yasar narrating from Zayd ibn Thabit that he said: For the standing eye poked is a tenth of the blood money, one hundred dinars."
"16901","From Ibn Jurayj, from Yahya, that he heard Said ibn al-Musayyib saying: For the standing eye poked is a tenth of the blood money."
"16902","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, that Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled for the blind one-eyed eye, when sunken, a third of its blood money."
"16903","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled for the standing eye, when injured and extinguished, a third of its blood money."
"16904","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, for the withered hand, the standing eye, the collarbone, the rib and the like is a judicial assessment."
"16905","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me that in a letter of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz: If the eye is slapped and sheds from its top tears that do not stop, then therein is two thirds of the blood money. If it is a tear whose flow does not dry, lesser than the first tear, then half the blood money of the eye. If it is a tear from the lid flowing at times, its sight gone in it, then therein is five hundred dinars. If it is a tear drying at times and flowing at others, harming him and injuring his sight, then a fifth of the blood money of the eye. If it is a tear from the lower eye with an eyelid, then upon that pattern, about one hundred dinars."
"16906","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to the commanders of the troops to write to him with the knowledge of their scholars. He said: And among what their jurists agreed upon, for the wink of the eye is a third of the blood money."
"16907","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to the commanders of the troops to write to him with the knowledge of their scholars. He said: And among what their jurists agreed upon, for the eye ridge is a third of the blood money."
"16908","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: How much for the nose uprooted. He said: The blood money."
"16909","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, he said: For the nose is the blood money, when uprooted."
"16910","From Mamar, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, wrote for them a letter, therein: And for the nose, when its cutting is complete, the full blood money, one hundred camels."
"16911","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled for the nose the blood money."
"16912","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: For the tip of the nose is a third of the blood money."
"16913","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, that he used to say: For the tip is the third, and if the soft part reaches the bone, then the full blood money. If injured of the tip, the nostril or other, so long as it does not reach the bone, then by reckoning of the tip."
"16914","From Mamar, from a man, from Ikrimah, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled for the nose, when all cut, the blood money, and when its tip cut, the half."
"16915","From Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman ibn Musa, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz said: For the nose, when its cutting is complete, the full blood money, and what is injured of the nose less than that is by its reckoning."
"16916","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ruled for the nose, when all cut, the full blood money, and when its tip cut, half the blood money, fifty camels, or its value in gold, silver, cattle or sheep."
"16917","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, he said: In the book with them, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: For the nose, when the soft part is cut, one hundred."
"16918","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, for the nose, when its cutting is complete, the full blood money, and what is injured of the nose less than that is by its reckoning, or its value in gold or silver. And for the nose of a woman, when completed, the full blood money, and what is injured of the nose less than that is by that reckoning in gold or silver."
"16919","From al-Thawri, from his father, from al-Shabi, he said: What is gone of the nose is by its reckoning."
"16920","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: For the nose is there a jaifah. He said: Yes."
"16921","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Abi Najih informed me, from Mujahid, he said: He used to say for the jaifah of the nose is a third of the blood money, and if it pierces through, then two thirds."
"16922","From Mamar, from Ata al-Khurasani, for the nose when pierced one hundred dinars. Mamar said: And I heard another saying: A third of the blood money. He says: It is a jaifah."
"16923","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman informed me: A slave broke one of the two tubes of the nose of a man, and that was brought to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, and Umar said: I found in a letter of Umar ibn al-Khattab: Whatever bone is broken, then sets as it was, therein is two hiqqahs. Ibn Suraqah reviewed him, he said: He only broke one of the two tubes. But Umar refused except to place therein the two hiqqahs."
"16924","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz said: If the nose is broken a break causing disfigurement, then a sixth of its blood money. If in the two nostrils of it is the disfigurement, then a third of the blood money of the two nostrils. If the soft nose is gouged a gouge, then for him a third of the blood money. If crushed and flattened, his voice hoarse like the eye, then half the blood money. For its probing and hoarseness five hundred dinars. If there is no defect, no deceit, no odor found from it, then for him a quarter of the blood money. If the tube of the nose is injured and pierces, with disfigurement, except he finds no stinking odor in it, then an eighth of the blood money, one hundred and twenty-five dinars. If he struck his nose and it healed without disfigurement, except he finds no pleasant odor nor stinking odor, then for him a tenth of the blood money, one hundred dinars."
"16925","I heard a freedman of Sulayman ibn Habib narrating, he said: Sulayman ibn Habib ruled for the nose, when pierced, ten dinars, and when broken, one hundred dinars."
"16926","Sufyan said: For the nose, when broken, is a judicial assessment."
"16927","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, concerning a man who plucked from the beard of another, he said: Retaliation is taken from him by weight, and what does not suffice is completed from the head hair. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, the same."
"16928","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The two lips. He said: Fifty camels."
"16929","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: For the two lips is the full blood money. Qatadah said: If one of them is cut, then half the blood money."
"16930","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: For the lower lip is two thirds of the blood money, and for the upper a third of the blood money."
"16931","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: For the two lips, they are equal, and the lower only exceeds in the ages of camels. And Qatadah said: They are equal."
"16932","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Abi Najih informed me, from Mujahid, for the two lips fifty fifty, and the lower exceeds the upper, for woman and man, in aggravation, not exceeding in number, but in the ages of camels."
"16933","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Asim informed me, from Yaqub ibn Asim, that Marwan ruled for the upper lip forty-five camels, and for the lower lip fifty-five."
"16934","From Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: Abu Bakr ruled: For the two lips the blood money, one hundred camels."
"16935","From al-Thawri, from Zakariya, from al-Shabi, he said: The two lips are equal."
"16936","From Israil, he said: Abu Ishaq informed me, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, he said: For the two lips is the blood money."
"16937","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: It used to be said for every single of man: the tongue, the nose and the like, the blood money, and for the two the blood money. I said: The two lips. He said: Perhaps that."
"16938","From Mamar, he said: It reached me for the two mustaches one hundred and twenty dinars, for each one sixty dinars."
"16939","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It was agreed for Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz that whoever tears out the mustache, therein is sixty dinars, and if both torn together, therein is one hundred and twenty dinars."
"16940","From Mamar, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, wrote for them a letter, therein: And for the tooth is five camels."
